Guide.content._register("w06", {"id":"w06","order":6,"region":"knowledge","title":"RAG Guardrails","short":"Guard the answer","tagline":"The model will answer anyway. Decide what it may say, what it may read and what it may do.","story":"The right chunks reached the model, and the model is about to answer. It will answer fluently whether or not the chunks support it. A retrieved page may carry hidden instructions, the user may probe for harmful output, and a tool call may leave the building. This world places guards around the whole path, prices their errors and chooses between platform services, open source and custom checks.","oneLiner":"Place guards on input, retrieval, tools and output, enforce grounding, and measure what each guard costs in latency and false alarms.","youWillLearn":["Place guards at input, retrieval, tool and output points and decide which run in parallel","Choose a streaming mode and explain what violating text can reach the user","Separate answerability, groundedness and citation validity, and enforce each one","Read Azure AI Content Safety and Foundry guardrails by status: GA, preview and retired","Choose between platform guards, NeMo Guardrails, Guardrails AI, Llama Guard and custom checks","Handle PII at ingest, at query time and in output","Price false positives and evaluate guards before users meet them"],"minutes":100,"prereqs":["w05"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w06-fc-001","front":"Name the places a guard can stand around a RAG call.","back":"User input, retrieved content, tool call, tool response and output. Foundry exposes input, tool call (preview), tool response (preview) and output. NeMo names input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output rails.","tags":["placement"]},{"id":"w06-fc-002","front":"Why do independent guards run in parallel?","back":"Parallel guards add only the slowest one's latency, while sequential guards add up. No vendor page checked on 2026-10-13 publishes guard latency, so measure each guard.","tags":["latency"]},{"id":"w06-fc-003","front":"What is the trade-off of Azure's asynchronous filter streaming mode?","back":"No filter latency and token-by-token streaming, but the stop signal is guaranteed only within about a 1,000-character window, so violating text can reach the user first.","tags":["streaming"]},{"id":"w06-fc-004","front":"What does the default streaming mode do with filtering?","back":"It buffers completion text, filters each buffer and returns content chunks, not token by token. It is safer than asynchronous mode and delivers the first chunk later.","tags":["streaming"]},{"id":"w06-fc-005","front":"How does a Foundry agent's guardrail relate to its model's guardrail?","back":"It fully overrides it. An agent guardrail with no tool-call or tool-response controls leaves those unscanned. With no custom guardrail the agent inherits the model's.","tags":["foundry"]},{"id":"w06-fc-006","front":"What are Prompt Shields' two shields?","back":"User prompt attacks (jailbreaks) and document (indirect) attacks. The standalone API takes one `userPrompt` and up to five `documents` and returns `attackDetected`. It was tested on English only.","tags":["content-safety"]},{"id":"w06-fc-007","front":"What does Azure Spotlighting do, and what does it cost?","back":"It marks documents as lower trust by base64 encoding them (preview, off by default, Chat Completions models only). Token counts rise and the model may mention base64.","tags":["content-safety"]},{"id":"w06-fc-008","front":"What does a citation prove?","back":"Provenance, not entailment. A cited span can still contradict the claim, so add a quote-first step or a claim-level support check.","tags":["grounding"]},{"id":"w06-fc-009","front":"Which three checks sit around grounding?","back":"Answerability before generation, groundedness after generation (is each claim entailed?) and citation validity (does each cited span exist and support the claim?).","tags":["grounding"]},{"id":"w06-fc-010","front":"What are the Azure groundedness detection limits?","back":"Optimised for English, grounding sources 55,000 characters, text plus query 7,500, query at least 3 words. Reasoning mode explains ungrounded segments and suits development.","tags":["grounding","content-safety"]},{"id":"w06-fc-011","front":"Name NeMo Guardrails' five rail types.","back":"Input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output. Retrieval rails filter chunks and execution rails check tool calls.","tags":["nemo"]},{"id":"w06-fc-012","front":"What happens to NeMo's `self check facts` rail with a reasoning model?","back":"If the model spends its `max_tokens` budget thinking, the rail fails closed: score 0.0 and the response is blocked. Raise the budget or use a non-reasoning checker.","tags":["nemo"]},{"id":"w06-fc-013","front":"Why does Llama Guard suit a custom policy?","back":"It is a classifier whose category text you can rewrite in the prompt, so a custom taxonomy needs no retraining. Llama Guard 4 is 12B, multimodal and runs on one GPU.","tags":["llama-guard"]},{"id":"w06-fc-014","front":"What does the Presidio documentation warn about?","back":"That it cannot guarantee it finds all PII. Test on your own formats, add custom recognizers and layer other controls.","tags":["pii"]},{"id":"w06-fc-015","front":"What is the scope of the Foundry PII filter?","back":"Model output only (preview). Entity types follow Azure Language categories, and the action is annotate or annotate and block, which blocks the whole output.","tags":["pii"]},{"id":"w06-fc-016","front":"Why is guard precision low even with a good classifier?","back":"Base rates. At 0.5% harmful traffic, 95% recall and a 2% false positive rate, only about 19% of blocks are real harm.","tags":["evaluation"]},{"id":"w06-fc-017","front":"Why start a guard in shadow mode (annotate only)?","back":"It measures real block rates and false positives on live traffic before any user is blocked, and shows which categories and languages need tuning.","tags":["evaluation"]},{"id":"w06-fc-018","front":"What is the status of Custom categories (standard) on 2026-10-13?","back":"Its retirement date, 2026-09-01, has passed. The concept page still says retiring and points to Foundry custom text classification. Confirm in the portal. Rapid is preview.","tags":["content-safety"]},{"id":"w06-fc-019","front":"What should a guard do on a timeout in a regulated assistant?","back":"Fail closed or degrade to abstain or escalate. Treating a timeout as a pass is a fail-open bug, and the guard timeout rate needs an alert.","tags":["reliability"]},{"id":"w06-fc-020","front":"What are the protected material limits worth remembering?","back":"Text and code detection are English only, the code index is current only through 2023-04-06, and completions need at least 110 characters to be scanned.","tags":["content-safety"]},{"id":"w06-t01-fc1","front":"Name the four Foundry guardrail intervention points.","back":"User input, tool call (preview, agents only), tool response (preview, agents only) and output.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t01"},{"id":"w06-t01-fc2","front":"Why scan retrieved chunks and tool responses?","back":"They are untrusted text that can carry instructions the user never typed, so input guards alone miss document attacks.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t01"},{"id":"w06-t01-fc3","front":"How do parallel guards affect latency?","back":"Independent parallel guards add roughly the slowest one. Sequential guards add the sum.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t01"},{"id":"w06-t02-fc1","front":"Groundedness versus correctness?","back":"Groundedness asks whether claims are supported by the supplied evidence. An answer can be grounded and still wrong if the evidence is wrong.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t02"},{"id":"w06-t02-fc2","front":"Azure groundedness detection limits?","back":"Preview, English only. Grounding sources 55,000 characters, text plus query 7,500 characters, query at least 3 words. Models, not agents.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t02"},{"id":"w06-t02-fc3","front":"Why check at claim level?","back":"A whole-answer score can hide one invented sentence. Per-claim entailment catches it (SummaC splits at sentence level and aggregates).","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t02"},{"id":"w06-t03-fc1","front":"Provenance versus entailment?","back":"A citation shows where text came from (provenance). Entailment asks whether the cited span supports the claim and needs its own check.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t03"},{"id":"w06-t03-fc2","front":"Which Azure reranker facts matter for abstention thresholds?","back":"Scores run 0 to 4, only the top 50 candidates are reranked and distributions shift with model updates. Use coarse cut-offs and re-test.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t03"},{"id":"w06-t03-fc3","front":"What do Anthropic citations not combine with?","back":"Structured outputs. Enabling both returns HTTP 400. They do work with prompt caching, token counting and batch.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t03"},{"id":"w06-t04-fc1","front":"Four harm categories in Azure AI Content Safety?","back":"Hate and fairness, sexual, violence and self-harm, for text and images.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t04"},{"id":"w06-t04-fc2","front":"Standalone severity scales?","back":"Text 0 to 7 (or trimmed 0, 2, 4, 6 with pairs merged). Image model returns only 0, 2, 4, 6.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t04"},{"id":"w06-t04-fc3","front":"What does the default guardrail do?","back":"`Microsoft.DefaultV2` sets the four harm categories to Medium on prompts and completions, and blocks with an error naming the category.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t04"},{"id":"w06-t05-fc1","front":"Two Prompt Shields attack families?","back":"User prompt attacks (formerly jailbreak detection) and document attacks (indirect), the latter hidden in retrieved or tool-returned content.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t05"},{"id":"w06-t05-fc2","front":"Standalone Prompt Shields request shape?","back":"One `userPrompt` and up to five `documents`. The response carries `attackDetected`. Prompt limit 10K characters.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t05"},{"id":"w06-t05-fc3","front":"Spotlighting trade-offs?","back":"Preview, base64 encodes documents, models only (not agents), raises tokens. Paper: attack success from above 50% to below 2%.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t05"},{"id":"w06-t06-fc1","front":"Groundedness detection limits?","back":"Sources 55,000 characters, text plus query 7,500, query at least 3 words, English only, preview, models not agents.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t06"},{"id":"w06-t06-fc2","front":"Protected material details?","back":"Text and code filters on completions, English only, minimum 110 characters. Code index current only through 2023-04-06.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t06"},{"id":"w06-t06-fc3","front":"Reasoning versus non-reasoning groundedness mode?","back":"Non-reasoning returns a fast verdict. Reasoning explains ungrounded segments, and the docs suggest it for development.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t06"},{"id":"w06-t07-fc1","front":"What is a Foundry guardrail?","back":"A named collection of controls. Each defines a risk, intervention points to scan and an action (annotate, or annotate and block).","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t07"},{"id":"w06-t07-fc2","front":"Agent versus model guardrail?","back":"An agent's guardrail fully overrides its model's. With none assigned, the agent inherits the model's. Applies only to agents built in Foundry Agent Service.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t07"},{"id":"w06-t07-fc3","front":"Default versus asynchronous streaming filter?","back":"Default buffers and filters each chunk. Asynchronous sends tokens immediately and signals stop only within about 1,000 characters.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t07"},{"id":"w06-t08-fc1","front":"Five NeMo Guardrails rail types?","back":"Input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output. Retrieval rails filter chunks, execution rails check tool calls.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t08"},{"id":"w06-t08-fc2","front":"Guardrails AI in one line?","back":"A Python framework running Input and Output Guards from Hub validators (65 on 2026-10-13), also producing structured output.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t08"},{"id":"w06-t08-fc3","front":"How do you encode a custom policy in Llama Guard?","back":"Rewrite the category text in the prompt, then evaluate on labelled data. It classifies text and image together, not an image alone.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t08"},{"id":"w06-t09-fc1","front":"Why is an output-only PII filter not enough?","back":"It scans model output only (preview). Personal data in prompts, tool responses and logs is already exposed.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t09"},{"id":"w06-t09-fc2","front":"Redaction, masking and tokenisation?","back":"Redaction removes the value, masking overwrites it, tokenisation swaps it for a placeholder and keeps a protected mapping so it can be restored.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t09"},{"id":"w06-t09-fc3","front":"What does Presidio's documentation warn about?","back":"There is no guarantee it finds all PII. Test your formats and languages, and add custom recognizers.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t09"},{"id":"w06-t10-fc1","front":"Why do most blocks hit benign users at low base rates?","back":"Benign traffic dwarfs harmful traffic, so even a 2% false positive rate produces more false blocks than true ones when harm is about 1%.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t10"},{"id":"w06-t10-fc2","front":"What does tiering guards mean?","back":"Order checks from cheap deterministic ones to classifiers to LLM judges, running costly ones only for high-risk or borderline cases.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t10"},{"id":"w06-t10-fc3","front":"What is shadow mode?","back":"Run the guard and log its decisions without acting, so you can measure false positives per category and language before enforcing.","tags":[],"topic":"w06-t10"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Guardrails and controls overview in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/guardrails-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Intervention points, actions, and agent versus model guardrail behaviour.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r2","title":"Default safety policies in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/guardrails/default-safety-policies","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"What Microsoft.DefaultV2 turns on by default.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"Prompt Shields","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/jailbreak-detection","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"User prompt attacks, document attacks, subtypes and Spotlighting.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Groundedness detection","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/groundedness","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Modes, domains, correction and the English-first limit.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r5","title":"Protected material detection","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/protected-material","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Text and code filters, English-only scope and the code index cut-off.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"Harm categories in Azure AI Content Safety","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/harm-categories","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Four categories, severity levels and scales.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r7","title":"Custom categories in Azure AI Content Safety","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/custom-categories","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Standard (retiring), rapid (preview) and the pointer to Foundry custom text classification.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r8","title":"Personally identifiable information filter in Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/content-filter-personal-information","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Output-only PII scanning with annotate or block.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r9","title":"Content streaming in Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/content-streaming","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Default and asynchronous filter modes and the 1,000-character window.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r10","title":"Azure AI Content Safety region availability, limits and languages","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/region-availability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Input limits and which features were tested on which languages.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r11","title":"How Microsoft defends against indirect prompt injection attacks","url":"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/msrc/blog/2025/07/how-microsoft-defends-against-indirect-prompt-injection-attacks","type":"blog","publisher":"Microsoft Security Response Center","note":"Defence in depth: hardening, Spotlighting, Prompt Shields and deterministic controls.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r12","title":"Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks With Spotlighting","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.14720","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Microsoft)","note":"The research behind Spotlighting and its measured effect.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r13","title":"NVIDIA NeMo Guardrails library overview","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/nemo/guardrails/about-nemo-guardrails-library/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"NVIDIA","note":"Input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output rails.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"NeMo Guardrails fact-checking rail","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/nemo/guardrails/latest/configure-guardrails/guardrail-catalog/fact-checking.md","type":"doc","publisher":"NVIDIA","note":"How self check facts scores grounding and when it fails closed.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r15","title":"Guardrails AI documentation","url":"https://guardrailsai.com/guardrails/docs","type":"doc","publisher":"Guardrails AI","note":"Input and output guards built from Hub validators.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r16","title":"Llama Guard 4 model card and prompt format","url":"https://www.llama.com/docs/model-cards-and-prompt-formats/llama-guard-4/","type":"doc","publisher":"Meta","note":"Open safety classifier, hazard categories and how to customise them.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r17","title":"Presidio documentation","url":"https://presidio.dataprivacystack.org/","type":"doc","publisher":"Data Privacy Stack (community)","note":"Analyzer, anonymizer, image redactor and the no-guarantee warning.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r18","title":"What is the Personally Identifiable Information detection feature in Azure AI Language?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/language-service/personally-identifiable-information/overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Text, conversation and document PII, categories and redaction.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r19","title":"SummaC: Re-Visiting NLI-based Models for Inconsistency Detection in Summarization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09525","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Sentence-level NLI aggregated into a consistency check.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"TRUE: Re-evaluating Factual Consistency Evaluation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.04991","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"A meta-evaluation of factual consistency metrics across tasks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r21","title":"AlignScore: Evaluating Factual Consistency with a Unified Alignment Function","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16739","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"A unified alignment model for entailment-style checks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"Enabling Large Language Models to Generate Text with Citations (ALCE)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14627","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"A benchmark for answers with citations.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"Reduce hallucinations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Permission to say I don't know and quote-first prompting.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r24","title":"Citations","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Native citations and the structured-output incompatibility.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r25","title":"Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.15884","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"A retrieval evaluator that gates generation and triggers fallbacks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"Implement generative AI guardrails with Azure AI Content Safety","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/moderate-content-detect-harm-azure-ai-content-safety/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Hands-on module on moderation, groundedness, protected material and Prompt Shields.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r27","title":"Safe and reliable AI via guardrails","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/safe-and-reliable-ai-via-guardrails","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Practical input and output guards with Guardrails AI.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r28","title":"NVIDIA NeMo Guardrails on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/NVIDIA-NeMo/Guardrails","type":"repo","publisher":"NVIDIA","note":"Source, examples and release notes for the library.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r29","title":"Guardrails AI on GitHub","url":"https://github.com/guardrails-ai/guardrails","type":"repo","publisher":"Guardrails AI","note":"Source and examples for the validator framework.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Semantic ranking in Azure AI Search","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-search-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Reranker scores run 0 to 4 and the ranker reorders the top 50 candidates, so score thresholds must be coarse and re-tested.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Query a knowledge base using the retrieve action or MCP endpoint","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/agentic-retrieval-how-to-retrieve","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Retrieve calls without a user identity header return unfiltered results from indexed sources, a fail-open retrieval risk (preview API).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Guardrail (Foundry)","def":"A named set of controls. Each control pairs a risk, an intervention point, a severity threshold and an action."},{"term":"Intervention point","def":"A place where a guardrail scans content: user input, tool call, tool response or output. Tool call and tool response are agent-only previews."},{"term":"Prompt Shields","def":"Azure AI Content Safety detectors for user prompt attacks (jailbreaks) and document (indirect) attacks."},{"term":"Indirect attack","def":"Instructions hidden in a document, web page, email or tool result that the model reads as if the user had written them."},{"term":"Spotlighting","def":"Marking untrusted text so the model can tell it from instructions. Azure's version base64 encodes documents and is a preview feature."},{"term":"Groundedness","def":"The share of an answer's claims that the supplied sources support. It is a property of each claim, not of the whole answer."},{"term":"Answerability","def":"Whether the retrieved evidence contains an answer at all. It is checked before generation."},{"term":"Entailment (NLI)","def":"A claim is entailed when the source text implies it. NLI models score this and back many groundedness checks."},{"term":"Protected material","def":"Known copyrighted text or licensed code that a completion reproduces. Azure detects text and code matches."},{"term":"Rail","def":"NeMo Guardrails' name for a guard: input, retrieval, dialog, execution or output."},{"term":"Fail closed","def":"When a guard cannot decide, the request is refused or sent to review. The opposite, fail open, lets it through."},{"term":"False positive","def":"A benign request or answer that a guard blocks. Its cost is lost help and user trust."},{"term":"Annotate versus block","def":"Annotate records a verdict and lets the content through. Block stops it. Annotate is how you run a guard in shadow mode."},{"term":"Llama Guard","def":"Meta's open safety classifier models. The prompt lists hazard categories, and you can rewrite that text for a custom taxonomy."},{"term":"Presidio","def":"An open-source toolkit that detects and anonymizes PII with recognizers that combine NER, regex, rules and context."},{"term":"Abstention","def":"Declining to answer, or answering that the evidence does not support a response, instead of guessing."},{"term":"Claim-level check","def":"Splitting an answer into individual claims and testing each against the evidence, which catches one bad sentence in an otherwise grounded answer."},{"term":"Severity threshold","def":"The setting (Off, Low, Medium, High) that decides how severe a harm-category detection must be before a guardrail control flags it."},{"term":"Asynchronous filter","def":"A streaming mode that sends tokens immediately and delivers filter annotations in separate messages, so violating text can appear briefly before the stop signal."},{"term":"Base rate","def":"The share of requests that are truly harmful. At low base rates even an accurate guard produces mostly false alarms."},{"term":"Shadow mode","def":"Running a guard and logging what it would do without acting, so you can measure false positives before enforcing."},{"term":"Tiered guards","def":"Ordering checks from cheap and deterministic to expensive and model based, and invoking the costly ones only for high-risk or borderline cases."},{"term":"Reversible redaction","def":"Replacing sensitive values with tokens and keeping the mapping in a protected store so authorised code can restore them in the response."},{"term":"Masking","def":"Overwriting sensitive characters, for example with an asterisk, so the value cannot be recovered from the output."},{"term":"Colang","def":"The flow language used with YAML configuration in NeMo Guardrails to define allowed dialog paths and actions."},{"term":"Answerability gate","def":"A check before generation that decides whether the retrieved evidence is sufficient to answer, and routes to abstention if not."}],"levels":[{"id":"w06-l1","order":1,"title":"Place the guards where the damage happens","kind":"problem","minutes":16,"brief":"The model will answer whatever it is given. Checks can stand on the way in, on retrieved content, on tool calls and on the way out. Which do you need, how many can the latency budget carry, and what may reach the user while text streams?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Five places a guard can stand","level":2,"id":"five-places-a-guard-can-stand"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w06-l1-path","title":"Guard points on a RAG request","alt":"A user input passes an input guard, retrieval returns chunks that pass a retrieval guard, the model proposes a tool call that passes a tool call guard, the tool response passes a tool response guard, and the final answer passes an output guard before reaching the user.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User input] --> IG{Input guard}\n  IG --> RT[Retrieve chunks]\n  RT --> RG{Retrieval guard}\n  RG --> M[Model]\n  M --> TG{Tool call guard}\n  TG --> T[Tool]\n  T --> TR{Tool response guard}\n  TR --> M\n  M --> OG{Output guard}\n  OG --> A[Answer]","caption":"Foundry names four intervention points (input, tool call, tool response, output). NeMo Guardrails names five rail types (input, retrieval, dialog, execution, output)."},{"t":"table","caption":"What each placement catches","head":["Placement","Catches","Typical control","Cost of skipping it"],"rows":[["Input","Jailbreaks, harmful requests, PII in prompts","Prompt Shields user prompt attacks, harm categories","Attacks reach the model unchallenged"],["Retrieval or tool response","Instructions hidden in documents, off-topic or poisoned chunks","Prompt Shields document attacks, chunk filters (NeMo retrieval rails)","Indirect injection rides in as trusted context"],["Tool call","Unsafe arguments, premature or misaligned tool use","Foundry tool call controls (preview), allow lists, task adherence (preview)","Side effects happen before anyone looks"],["Output","Harmful text, ungrounded claims, protected material, PII","Harm categories, groundedness, protected material, PII filter","The user sees the failure"],["Audit after the fact","Patterns, drift, missed attacks","Sampled review, logs, evals","No learning loop"]],"highlightCol":3},{"t":"compare","title":"How guards run in the request path","intro":"Where a guard stands is one decision. Whether it blocks the request, races it or trails behind a stream is another. Nothing in the vendor pages checked on 2026-10-13 publishes guard latency, so every option here is measured, not assumed.","options":[{"name":"Sequential gate before the model","summary":"Run the input guard, then call the model.","pros":["No tokens are spent on blocked requests","Easiest to reason about"],"cons":["The guard's latency adds in series"],"bestWhen":"Blocked requests are common or generation is costly.","avoidWhen":"The latency budget is tight and blocks are rare.","meta":[{"k":"Latency added","v":"the guard's full time"}]},{"name":"Parallel with generation","summary":"Start the guard and the model together, cancel generation if the guard blocks.","pros":["Hides the guard's latency behind the model call","Independent guards add only the slowest one"],"cons":["Wasted tokens when the guard blocks","No tool side effects until the verdict arrives"],"bestWhen":"Blocks are rare and the budget is tight.","avoidWhen":"Generation triggers tools or other side effects.","meta":[{"k":"Latency added","v":"about the slowest guard, minus overlap"}]},{"name":"Buffered streaming (Azure default)","tag":"GA","summary":"Buffer completion text, filter each buffer and return content chunks.","pros":["Violating text is stopped before it is sent","No extra code"],"cons":["Chunky streaming, not token by token","The first chunk arrives later"],"bestWhen":"Harm if text leaks is high.","avoidWhen":"Smooth token streaming is a hard product requirement.","meta":[{"k":"User sees","v":"filtered chunks"}]},{"name":"Asynchronous filter (Azure)","tag":"GA","summary":"Stream token by token and deliver annotations in separate messages with offsets.","pros":["No filter latency","Token-by-token streaming"],"cons":["The stop signal is guaranteed only within about a 1,000-character window, so violating text can reach the user first","Changing the mode needs permission to modify content filtering policies"],"bestWhen":"Brief exposure is tolerable and you can retract or hide content.","avoidWhen":"Any shown violation is unacceptable.","meta":[{"k":"User sees","v":"tokens, then a verdict"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w06-l1-stack","title":"Pick a guard stack","prompt":"Flip the risk tier, the latency budget and how the answer is delivered.","dials":[{"id":"risk","label":"Risk tier","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Internal, low harm"},{"id":"mid","label":"Customer-facing"},{"id":"high","label":"Regulated or safety-relevant"}]},{"id":"latency","label":"Latency budget","values":[{"id":"tight","label":"Tight"},{"id":"relaxed","label":"Relaxed"}]},{"id":"delivery","label":"Delivery","values":[{"id":"stream","label":"Tokens stream to the user"},{"id":"whole","label":"Whole answer after checks"}]}],"options":[{"id":"det","name":"Deterministic checks only","summary":"Schemas, allow lists, regex, size limits."},{"id":"platform","name":"Platform filters on input and output","summary":"Harm categories, Prompt Shields, protected material."},{"id":"ground","name":"Platform plus a groundedness check","summary":"Adds a claim-level check on the answer."},{"id":"full","name":"Full path: input, retrieval, tools, output","summary":"Guards on every placement."},{"id":"judge","name":"LLM judge on every answer","summary":"A second model reviews each answer against a policy."}],"scores":{"det":{"risk":{"low":{"s":2,"why":"Cheap and enough when harm is low."},"mid":{"s":0,"why":"It misses semantic harms and ungrounded claims."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"It cannot judge meaning, so it cannot carry a regulated assistant."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":2,"why":"Microseconds of work, no model call."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Spare latency goes unused."}},"delivery":{"stream":{"s":1,"why":"Nothing to buffer for."},"whole":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"platform":{"risk":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Defaults are enough and cost little."},"mid":{"s":2,"why":"Covers the common harms and jailbreaks."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Necessary but not sufficient: no claim check and no tool scans by default."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":1,"why":"One classifier call, which you should measure, since no vendor figure is published."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Fits within the budget."}},"delivery":{"stream":{"s":0,"why":"Default mode buffers chunks, asynchronous mode trades exposure for speed."},"whole":{"s":1,"why":"Output checks run on the full text."}}},"ground":{"risk":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"More than the harm warrants."},"mid":{"s":1,"why":"Cuts confident wrong answers."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Adds an answer-level check, but still no tool coverage."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-1,"why":"Needs the finished answer and a second call."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Affordable."}},"delivery":{"stream":{"s":-2,"why":"It must see the answer before judging it, so it conflicts with streaming."},"whole":{"s":2,"why":"Checks run before anything is shown."}}},"full":{"risk":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Overbuilt and costly to tune."},"mid":{"s":0,"why":"Reasonable if documents or tools are untrusted."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Covers input, retrieved content, tool calls and output."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-2,"why":"Many guards, even in parallel, add the slowest of each stage."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"The budget can carry it."}},"delivery":{"stream":{"s":-1,"why":"Output checks fight streaming."},"whole":{"s":1,"why":"Fits a check-then-show flow."}}},"judge":{"risk":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Costs a full model call per answer for little gain."},"mid":{"s":-1,"why":"Heavy for the benefit."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Flexible for custom policy, but not deterministic."}},"latency":{"tight":{"s":-2,"why":"One extra full LLM call, roughly seconds (author estimate, not a vendor figure)."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Possible if the budget allows."}},"delivery":{"stream":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs the whole answer first."},"whole":{"s":1,"why":"Fits."}}}},"takeaway":"Risk tier and delivery mode usually decide before latency does. Streaming and answer-level checks pull in opposite directions."},{"t":"failure","title":"Guard placement failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Policy-violating text flashes on screen and then disappears.","cause":"Asynchronous filter mode streams first and signals the stop later, within about 1,000 characters.","fix":"Use buffered streaming for high-risk surfaces, or hold display until the verdict. Detect with annotation offsets in logs."},{"symptom":"p95 latency equals the sum of five guard calls.","cause":"Independent guards run in series.","fix":"Run independent guards in parallel so you pay the slowest, and record per-guard timings."},{"symptom":"Tool calls go unscanned even though the model deployment has strict filters.","cause":"An agent's guardrail fully overrides its model's, and it has no tool call or tool response controls.","fix":"Add tool call and tool response controls (preview) to the agent's guardrail and test with a tool-bound attack."},{"symptom":"A poisoned PDF steers the model although the user prompt was clean.","cause":"Only user input was scanned. The attack arrived through retrieved content.","fix":"Scan retrieved chunks and tool responses for document attacks and treat them as untrusted."},{"symptom":"Guard outage lets everything through.","cause":"A timeout is treated as a pass, which fails open.","fix":"Choose fail closed or degrade to abstain per risk tier, and alert on guard timeouts."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Estimates in this level","md":"Vendors publish no guard latency numbers on the pages checked 2026-10-13. As a planning estimate only, a classifier call costs tens to a few hundred milliseconds and an LLM judge adds one full model call. Replace both with your own p50 and p95 measurements."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-l1-q1","type":"match","tag":"security","difficulty":1,"q":"Match each threat to the earliest guard placement that can stop it.","pairs":[{"l":"A user types a jailbreak prompt","r":"Input guard"},{"l":"Instructions hidden in a retrieved PDF","r":"Retrieval or tool response guard"},{"l":"An agent proposes a destructive API call","r":"Tool call guard"},{"l":"The model reproduces song lyrics","r":"Output guard"}],"explain":"Each threat is only visible at one point. A clean user prompt hides the PDF attack, a tool call exists before any output, and lyrics only appear in the completion. That is why a single guard on input is not a stack."},{"id":"w06-l1-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"A team switches Azure content filtering to the asynchronous filter mode to cut latency. Which statement is true?","options":["Annotations arrive before any text is streamed, so nothing is shown first","The filter buffers each chunk, so no violating text can ever be shown","Some violating text can be displayed before the stop signal arrives","The mode removes the need to configure any guardrail for the deployment"],"answer":2,"explain":"Asynchronous filter streams token by token and sends annotations in separate messages, so some violating text can be displayed before the stop signal, which is guaranteed only within about a 1,000-character window. The default mode is the one that buffers and filters each chunk. Guardrails still need configuring, and annotations follow the text, they do not precede it."},{"id":"w06-l1-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"Treat these p95 times as additive. Retrieval 400 ms, model 1,600 ms, input guard 120 ms. Three independent output guards take 80, 150 and 200 ms. Run in parallel, what is the total request latency?","answer":2320,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"ms","hint":"Parallel guards add only the slowest one","explain":"Input guard 120 + retrieval 400 + model 1,600 + the slowest output guard 200 = 2,320 ms. Run in series the output guards add 430 ms and the total is 2,550 ms. Summing p95 values is a planning shortcut, so confirm with a measured end-to-end p95."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r9","r13","r3"],"takeaways":["A guard only sees what passes its placement, so cover input, retrieved content, tools and output","Independent guards run in parallel add the slowest, not the sum","Asynchronous filter trades latency for brief exposure, with the stop signal guaranteed only within about 1,000 characters","An agent's guardrail replaces its model's guardrail, so check tool coverage explicitly"]},{"id":"w06-l2","order":2,"title":"Grounded or silent: make every claim earn its place","kind":"problem","minutes":17,"brief":"The retriever handed over three chunks and the model wrote five confident sentences. Which of them does the evidence support, and what should happen to the rest?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three checks that sound alike and are not","level":2,"id":"three-checks-that-sound-alike-and-are-not"},{"t":"table","caption":"Answerability, groundedness and citation validity","head":["Check","Question it asks","Runs","Typical signal","Catches"],"rows":[["Answerability","Does the evidence contain an answer at all?","Before generation","Reranker score cutoff, a retrieval evaluator such as CRAG [[r25]], an answerability instruction","Confident answers built on weak retrieval"],["Groundedness","Is each claim entailed by the evidence?","After generation","NLI model, LLM judge, Azure groundedness detection [[r4]]","Invented or distorted claims"],["Citation validity","Does each cited span exist and support the claim?","After generation","Id and span lookup plus entailment on that span","Fake or mismatched citations"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"compare","title":"Ways to enforce grounding","intro":"Pick by the cost of a wrong answer, the document length and the language. Each one also adds tokens or latency.","options":[{"name":"Instruction only","summary":"Tell the model to answer from the sources, to cite, and that it may say it does not know.","pros":["No extra call","Anthropic guidance recommends explicit permission to say I don't know [[r23]]"],"cons":["Not a guarantee, because the model can still over-claim"],"bestWhen":"Low risk, or as the first layer under stronger checks.","avoidWhen":"It is the only control in a regulated setting."},{"name":"Quote first, then answer","summary":"For long documents, ask for word-for-word quotes before the answer.","pros":["Anthropic suggests it for documents over 20k tokens","Makes the evidence visible and checkable"],"cons":["A second step adds latency and tokens","Quotes can be correct and misused"],"bestWhen":"Long source documents and a need to audit the evidence.","avoidWhen":"Latency is tight and sources are short."},{"name":"NLI or judge faithfulness check","summary":"Check every claim against its evidence with an entailment model or a prompted LLM.","pros":["SummaC scores sentence-level NLI and aggregates it [[r19]]","AlignScore is a unified alignment function trained on many tasks [[r21]]","TRUE meta-evaluates factual consistency metrics across tasks, which helps you pick one [[r20]]","NeMo's `self check facts` rail scores 0.0 to 1.0 against `$relevant_chunks` [[r14]]"],"cons":["A second pass adds latency","A judge that shares the generator's blind spots misses the same errors","Quality depends on the model following the check prompt"],"bestWhen":"Claims carry real cost and you can run a second pass.","avoidWhen":"You stream tokens and cannot hold the answer."},{"name":"Azure groundedness detection","tag":"Preview","summary":"A Content Safety API that checks an answer against supplied grounding sources and can optionally correct it.","pros":["Domain (`Medical`, `Generic`) and task (`Summarization`, `QnA`) options","Non-reasoning mode returns a fast verdict, reasoning mode explains ungrounded segments","Optional correction (preview)"],"cons":["Accuracy is optimised for English","Available only in specific regions","In Foundry guardrails it applies to models, not agents"],"bestWhen":"English content on Azure, and a managed claim check is acceptable.","avoidWhen":"Content is mostly non-English, or the agent surface is the target.","meta":[{"k":"Limits","v":"55,000 characters of sources, 7,500 characters of text plus query, query of 3 words or more"}]},{"name":"Retrieval-confidence abstention","summary":"Do not generate when the evidence is weak.","pros":["Cheapest, runs before generation","Pairs with a reranker score cutoff or a CRAG-style evaluator"],"cons":["Does not check the claims in an answer that was generated","Cutoffs drift when scoring models update"],"bestWhen":"Many questions are unanswerable and a refusal is acceptable.","avoidWhen":"Used as the only grounding control."}]},{"t":"flow","title":"A grounded answer pipeline","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Gate","md":"Is the question answerable from the evidence?","tag":"before"},{"title":"Generate","md":"Quote first or native citations, with permission to say it does not know.","tag":"LLM"},{"title":"Validate","md":"Every cited id and span exists in the retrieved set.","tag":"code"},{"title":"Check","md":"Each claim is entailed by its span.","tag":"NLI or judge"},{"title":"Respond","md":"Keep supported claims, repair once or abstain with what was found.","tag":"after"}]},{"t":"formula","title":"Pricing a cutoff","expr":"cost per query = P(wrong answer shipped) x C_wrong + P(false abstention) x C_abstain","vars":[{"k":"P(wrong answer shipped)","v":"share of answers that are wrong and reach the user"},{"k":"P(false abstention)","v":"share of answerable questions the system refuses"},{"k":"C_wrong, C_abstain","v":"your cost for each error, in any consistent unit"}],"example":"Raising a cutoff cuts wrong answers from 4% to 1.5% and raises false abstentions from 3% to 9%. With C_wrong = 20 and C_abstain = 2, cost per query falls from 0.04 x 20 + 0.03 x 2 = **0.86** to 0.015 x 20 + 0.09 x 2 = **0.48**. A cheaper wrong answer flips this."},{"t":"failure","title":"Grounding failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Citations resolve and the claim is still wrong.","cause":"Citation validity checks that a source exists, not that it supports the claim.","fix":"Add a claim-level entailment check against the cited span, and sample it with human review."},{"symptom":"Groundedness verdicts look odd on non-English answers.","cause":"Azure groundedness detection is optimised for English and does not reject other languages.","fix":"Calibrate a judge or NLI model per language, and test each language slice."},{"symptom":"Easy questions are refused.","cause":"The cutoff or answerability prompt is too tight.","fix":"Build a labelled set of answerable and unanswerable questions and track false abstentions next to wrong answers."},{"symptom":"Every answer is blocked after switching to a reasoning model.","cause":"NeMo's `self check facts` fails closed with score 0.0 when the model spends its `max_tokens` before answering.","fix":"Raise the token budget or use a non-reasoning model for the check."},{"symptom":"Requests with citations on and a JSON schema return HTTP 400.","cause":"Anthropic citations are incompatible with structured outputs.","fix":"Choose one, or add the citations in a second step."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"A citation is provenance, not proof","md":"A reference tells the reader where text came from. It does not show that the sentence follows from it. ALCE benchmarks answers with citations [[r22]], and the guardrail is a separate check that compares claim and span."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-l2-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"A cutoff change lowers wrong answers from 5% to 2% and raises false abstentions from 4% to 10%. With C_wrong = 25 and C_abstain = 3, what is the expected cost per query after the change?","answer":0.8,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"cost units","hint":"0.02 x 25 + 0.10 x 3","explain":"After the change the cost is 0.02 x 25 + 0.10 x 3 = 0.5 + 0.3 = 0.8. Before it was 0.05 x 25 + 0.04 x 3 = 1.37, so the change is worth it at these costs. If C_wrong were close to C_abstain, the extra refusals would outweigh the saved errors."},{"id":"w06-l2-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"This check log shows a passing citation and a wrong claim. Which control is missing?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"claim:       Refunds are accepted within 60 days [2]\ncited span:  Refunds are accepted within 30 days of delivery.\nid [2] in retrieved set: pass"},"options":["Lower the temperature so that the model copies the source more closely","Add a claim-level entailment check against the cited span","Increase k so that more chunks can be cited for each claim","Validate that every cited id exists in the retrieved set"],"answer":1,"explain":"The id check already passes, so it cannot be the missing control. The claim contradicts the span, which only an entailment check on the claim and its span detects. Temperature and k change what the model sees or how random it is, not whether a claim is checked."},{"id":"w06-l2-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every true statement about Azure groundedness detection as of 2026-10-13.","options":["Accuracy is optimised for English, although the API does not reject other languages","Reasoning mode explains ungrounded segments and non-reasoning mode returns a fast verdict","In Foundry guardrails the groundedness control also applies to agents","It is generally available in every Azure region"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"The docs describe both language behaviour and the two modes. The Foundry overview says previews such as groundedness are not yet supported for agents, so a control on an agent does not take effect. The feature is labelled preview and is available only in specific regions."},{"id":"w06-l2-q4","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":1,"q":"Put the steps of a grounded answer pipeline in order.","items":["Gate on evidence: is the question answerable?","Generate with quote-first or native citations","Validate that cited ids and spans exist","Check each claim for entailment","Respond, repair once or abstain"],"explain":"Gate first so you spend no generation on unanswerable questions. Citation ids are cheap to validate before the costlier entailment pass. The response decision comes last because it needs every verdict."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r4","r10","r14","r19","r21","r22","r23","r24","r25","r20"],"takeaways":["Answerability, groundedness and citation validity are three different checks","A citation proves provenance, not entailment","Price the cutoff: wrong answers and false abstentions both cost something","Azure groundedness detection is preview, English-optimised and limited by region as of 2026-10-13"]},{"id":"w06-l3","order":3,"title":"Read the platform guards by status","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Azure AI Content Safety and Foundry guardrails ship classifiers for harm, jailbreaks, document attacks, protected material and groundedness. Which are on by default, which are preview or retired, and where does each stop covering you?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"What the platform gives you, with status on 2026-10-13","level":2,"id":"what-the-platform-gives-you-with-status-on-2026-10-13"},{"t":"table","caption":"Azure AI Content Safety and Foundry guardrail features","head":["Capability","What it does and limits","Status"],"rows":[["Harm categories","Hate and fairness, sexual, violence, self-harm, for text and images. Foundry guardrails use four levels (safe is annotated and never filtered, then low, medium, high) with thresholds Off, Low, Medium or High [[r6]]","GA"],["Default guardrail `Microsoft.DefaultV2`","Hate, violence, sexual and self-harm at Medium on prompts and completions, user prompt attack on prompts, protected material text and code on completions. It covers Foundry Models sold by Azure except audio transcription, and every setting is configurable [[r2]] [[r1]]","GA"],["Prompt Shields","User prompt attacks (change system rules, conversation mockup, role-play, encoding) and document attacks. The standalone API takes one `userPrompt` and up to five `documents` and returns `attackDetected`. In Foundry, user attacks are scanned at input and document attacks at input and tool response [[r3]]","GA since Aug 2024, tested on English only [[r10]]"],["Spotlighting","Tags documents as lower trust by base64 encoding them. Off by default, Chat Completions only, models only. Raises token counts, and the model may mention base64. A Microsoft paper reports attack success falling from above 50% to below 2% on GPT-family models [[r12]]","Preview"],["Groundedness detection","Checks an answer against supplied sources, with optional correction. English-optimised and available in specific regions [[r4]]","Preview"],["Protected material","Text filter flags known text such as lyrics, articles and recipes. Code filter flags matches to GitHub repositories, with an index current only through 2023-04-06. English only, and completions need at least 110 characters [[r5]]","Text GA since Aug 2024, code listed without a Preview label in the Foundry controls table"],["Custom categories","Standard trains a text-only model and its retirement date, 2026-09-01, has passed while the concept page still says retiring and points to Foundry custom text classification. Rapid uses an LLM for emerging incidents on text and images [[r7]]","Standard retired or retiring, rapid preview"],["Task adherence","Flags misaligned or premature tool use [[r1]]","Preview"],["PII filter","Scans model output only for personal data, with annotate or annotate and block [[r8]]","Preview"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Prompt Shields input","value":"10K chars, 5 docs","note":"Prompt up to 10K characters, up to five documents with 10K characters in total."},{"label":"Analyze text API","value":"10K chars","note":"Default maximum. Split longer text."},{"label":"Groundedness sources","value":"55,000 chars","note":"Per call, with text plus query up to 7,500 characters (preview API)."},{"label":"Protected material minimum","value":"110 chars","note":"For scanning completions, not user prompts."}]},{"t":"compare","title":"Where to apply the controls","intro":"The same classifiers can be attached at three levels. The level decides what is scanned and who owns the failure.","options":[{"name":"Model deployment guardrail","tag":"GA","summary":"A guardrail attached to the model deployment, with `Microsoft.DefaultV2` by default.","pros":["Covers every app that calls the deployment","Default controls need no work"],"cons":["No tool call or tool response controls","Does not follow your agent logic"],"bestWhen":"Prompt-in, answer-out apps with no tools.","avoidWhen":"Agents with tools or untrusted tool results."},{"name":"Agent guardrail (Foundry Agent Service)","tag":"Preview","summary":"A guardrail assigned to an agent, with tool call and tool response intervention points.","pros":["Scans tool calls and tool responses (preview)","Hosted agents also get network egress controls (preview)"],"cons":["It fully overrides the model's guardrail, so omissions are silent","Applies only to agents built in Foundry Agent Service","Annotate is not available for agents, only annotate and block","Preview risks such as Spotlighting and Groundedness are not yet supported for agents"],"bestWhen":"Agents built in Foundry Agent Service that call tools.","avoidWhen":"Agents run elsewhere, since registering them in the Foundry Control Plane does not apply the guardrail."},{"name":"Your own calls to the Content Safety APIs","tag":"GA and preview mix","summary":"Call Prompt Shields, protected material or groundedness from your own code.","pros":["Any runtime and any placement","You control parallelism and fail-closed rules"],"cons":["You own limits, retries and latency","Each API has its own character limits"],"bestWhen":"Custom orchestration, non-Foundry runtimes, or checks at points the platform does not expose.","avoidWhen":"A platform guardrail already covers the need."}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w06-l3-agent","title":"Foundry agent with a guardrail at each intervention point","alt":"A prompt from the user passes an input scan, the model proposes a tool call that passes a tool call scan, the tool response passes a document attack scan, and the draft answer passes an output scan before reaching the user.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant U as User\n  participant G as Guardrail\n  participant M as Model\n  participant T as Tool\n  U->>G: Prompt\n  G->>M: Passes input scan\n  M->>G: Proposed tool call\n  G->>T: Passes tool call scan\n  T->>G: Tool response\n  G->>M: Passes document attack scan\n  M->>G: Draft answer\n  G->>U: Passes output scan","caption":"Tool call and tool response scanning are preview features for agents, checked 2026-10-13."},{"t":"failure","title":"Platform guard failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"A tool response carries an injected instruction and nothing flags it.","cause":"The agent's guardrail fully overrides the model's and has no tool response control.","fix":"Add the tool response intervention point (preview) with document attack detection, and test with a planted instruction."},{"symptom":"A control added to an agent's guardrail never fires.","cause":"Preview risks such as Spotlighting and Groundedness are not yet supported for agents, so the control does not take effect for the agent.","fix":"Read the supported-risk list before assuming coverage, and test the agent, not the model."},{"symptom":"A very long prompt is only partly moderated.","cause":"Limits apply. The Content Safety page says Foundry guardrails moderate the first 1,000 characters for text scenarios, and its exact scope is unclear. Standalone calls cap at 10K characters.","fix":"Test with long inputs on your own deployment, split text for standalone calls and cap input size deterministically."},{"symptom":"Non-English jailbreaks pass.","cause":"Prompt Shields, protected material and groundedness were tested on English only.","fix":"Build per-language red-team sets and add a layer you can tune."},{"symptom":"Leadership says content filters stop prompt injection.","cause":"Prompt Shields is a probabilistic detector, not a complete defence.","fix":"Layer prompt hardening, Spotlighting, Prompt Shields, least-privilege tools and deterministic blocking of exfiltration paths, as the MSRC defence-in-depth post describes [[r11]]."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Say the status, and the date","md":"Features move between preview, GA and retired. As of 2026-10-13, Prompt Shields is GA, groundedness, Spotlighting, task adherence, PII filtering and agent tool controls are preview, and Custom categories (standard) is past its 2026-09-01 retirement date. Name the status and the date you checked in an interview answer, and confirm in the portal before you promise a feature."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-l3-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","difficulty":3,"q":"A web search tool returns a page with an injected instruction. The model deployment's guardrail scans documents, but the agent has a custom guardrail with only user input and output controls. Why is the injection not scanned?","options":["Prompt Shields cannot scan documents at all, only user prompts","Tool responses are scanned only when the model deployment also sets a severity threshold for them","Document attack scanning is skipped for any content that was not written in English","The agent's guardrail fully overrides the model's and contains no tool response control"],"answer":3,"explain":"An agent is checked against the guardrail assigned to it, not its model's, and this one has no tool response control. Prompt Shields does have a document attack shield, the model's thresholds do not carry over, and English-only testing is a separate limitation that does not explain a missing control."},{"id":"w06-l3-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every true statement about Prompt Shields.","options":["It has two shields: user prompt attacks and document (indirect) attacks","The standalone API takes one `userPrompt` and up to five `documents`","It removes the need for least-privilege tool permissions","It was tested on English only, so test other languages yourself"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The docs describe the two shields, the call shape and the English-only testing. A probabilistic detector never replaces least privilege, which limits damage when a detection is missed."},{"id":"w06-l3-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Match each feature to its status as of 2026-10-13.","pairs":[{"l":"Prompt Shields","r":"GA since August 2024"},{"l":"Groundedness detection","r":"Preview, English-optimised, specific regions"},{"l":"Custom categories (standard)","r":"Retirement date 2026-09-01 has passed, confirm in the portal"},{"l":"Agent tool call controls","r":"Preview, agents only"}],"explain":"Status changes what you can promise. The custom categories page still says retiring after its date passed, which is why the portal is the source of truth."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5","r6","r7","r8","r10","r11","r12"],"takeaways":["Prompt Shields is GA, and groundedness, Spotlighting, PII filtering and agent tool controls are preview as of 2026-10-13","An agent's guardrail replaces its model's, so tool coverage must be added explicitly","Several features were tested on English only, so build per-language tests","Content filters are one layer, not a defence against prompt injection"]},{"id":"w06-l4","order":4,"title":"Build, borrow or buy: open-source, custom and PII guards","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"The platform guards cover the common harms. Your taxonomy, your languages or your data-residency rules may say otherwise. When do you reach for NeMo Guardrails, Guardrails AI, Llama Guard, plain deterministic checks or a PII detector?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four kinds of guard you can run yourself","level":2,"id":"four-kinds-of-guard-you-can-run-yourself"},{"t":"compare","title":"Open-source and custom guards","intro":"Version and catalogue facts were checked on 2026-10-13 and change often, so pin versions and re-test.","options":[{"name":"NeMo Guardrails","tag":"Open source","summary":"A Python library, also a Kubernetes microservice, configured with YAML and Colang.","pros":["Five rail types: input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output. Retrieval rails filter chunks and execution rails check tool calls [[r13]]","Built-in content safety, jailbreak detection, topic control, PII handling and fact checking, plus integrations such as Llama Guard","Runs wherever you host it"],"cons":["You operate it","LLM-based rails depend on prompt quality, and `self check facts` fails closed with score 0.0 if a reasoning model uses its whole `max_tokens` budget [[r14]]"],"bestWhen":"You need dialog and flow control as well as classification, or data must stay on your servers.","avoidWhen":"You only need a managed harm filter."},{"name":"Guardrails AI","tag":"Open source","summary":"A Python framework that runs Input and Output Guards built from validators in Guardrails Hub, and can also produce structured output.","pros":["The Hub listed 65 validators on 2026-10-13, for example ban list, toxic language, detect jailbreak and detect PII (which uses Presidio) [[r15]]","Fits code-first teams and structured output checks"],"cons":["Validator quality and dependencies vary","The catalogue count changes often"],"bestWhen":"You want to compose small, named checks in application code.","avoidWhen":"You need a policy engine across dialog turns."},{"name":"Llama Guard","tag":"Open weights","summary":"A safety classifier that labels prompts and responses against hazard categories.","pros":["Llama Guard 4 is 12B parameters, natively multimodal and runs on one GPU, as a drop-in for Llama Guard 3 8B and 11B","Category text can be rewritten in the prompt, so a custom taxonomy needs no retraining","Llama Guard 3 1B stays useful on the edge [[r16]]"],"cons":["You host a GPU model and add a hop","It classifies prompt text and image together, not an image alone","Broad categories such as S6 Specialized Advice can over-block legitimate regulated content"],"bestWhen":"You need your own taxonomy and the text must stay in your network.","avoidWhen":"You cannot host a model or tune category text."},{"name":"Deterministic checks","summary":"Schemas, allow lists, regex, size caps, URL and domain allow lists, output format validation.","pros":["Fast, predictable and auditable","The right tool for blocking exfiltration paths, as the MSRC defence-in-depth post notes [[r11]]"],"cons":["No semantic judgement","Brittle against rephrasing"],"bestWhen":"A rule can express the policy exactly.","avoidWhen":"The policy needs meaning, such as tone or intent."}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w06-l4-pick","title":"Platform service, open source or custom?","prompt":"Flip where text may go, how custom your policy is and which languages you serve.","dials":[{"id":"data","label":"Where text may go","values":[{"id":"managed","label":"A managed service is fine"},{"id":"own","label":"Only our own servers"}]},{"id":"taxonomy","label":"Policy shape","values":[{"id":"standard","label":"Standard harm categories"},{"id":"custom","label":"Custom policy categories"}]},{"id":"language","label":"Languages","values":[{"id":"english","label":"English"},{"id":"many","label":"Many languages"}]}],"options":[{"id":"foundry","name":"Foundry guardrails and Content Safety","summary":"Managed classifiers and a policy surface."},{"id":"nemo","name":"NeMo Guardrails","summary":"Open-source rails and flows you host."},{"id":"gai","name":"Guardrails AI validators","summary":"Composable Hub validators in your code."},{"id":"lg","name":"Llama Guard (self-hosted)","summary":"Open safety classifier with editable categories."},{"id":"custom","name":"Custom classifier or LLM judge","summary":"A policy you define and label yourself."}],"scores":{"foundry":{"data":{"managed":{"s":2,"why":"No model to host, and it sits in your Azure estate."},"own":{"s":-2,"why":"Text goes to a managed service."}},"taxonomy":{"standard":{"s":2,"why":"Harm categories, jailbreak and protected material are built in."},"custom":{"s":0,"why":"Custom categories (standard) is retired or retiring and rapid is preview."}},"language":{"english":{"s":2,"why":"Best coverage."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"Harm models were tested on eight languages and several features on English only."}}},"nemo":{"data":{"managed":{"s":0,"why":"It orchestrates whichever models you choose."},"own":{"s":2,"why":"Runs in your process or as a microservice on your Kubernetes."}},"taxonomy":{"standard":{"s":1,"why":"Built-in content safety, jailbreak, topic control, PII and fact-check rails."},"custom":{"s":2,"why":"Colang flows and actions express your policy."}},"language":{"english":{"s":0,"why":"Depends on the models you plug in."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Same dependency, so test each language."}}},"gai":{"data":{"managed":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"own":{"s":1,"why":"Validators run in your process, though some call external services."}},"taxonomy":{"standard":{"s":1,"why":"Hub validators cover ban lists, toxicity, jailbreak and PII."},"custom":{"s":1,"why":"Compose validators or write your own."}},"language":{"english":{"s":0,"why":"Validator dependent."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Validator dependent, so test each language."}}},"lg":{"data":{"managed":{"s":0,"why":"You host it either way."},"own":{"s":2,"why":"Open weights on your own GPU."}},"taxonomy":{"standard":{"s":1,"why":"Llama Guard 3 ships S1 to S14 hazard categories."},"custom":{"s":2,"why":"Rewrite the category text in the prompt."}},"language":{"english":{"s":0,"why":"Check the model card for your case."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Test your languages, because coverage is not assumed here."}}},"custom":{"data":{"managed":{"s":1,"why":"Call any model API you approve."},"own":{"s":1,"why":"Possible with an open model."}},"taxonomy":{"standard":{"s":-1,"why":"Rebuilding what exists costs more."},"custom":{"s":2,"why":"You define the policy and the labelled data exactly."}},"language":{"english":{"s":0,"why":"No advantage."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"You can prompt or tune per language, at a cost."}}}},"takeaway":"Data location and policy shape decide most of this. Language coverage is the dial people forget to test."},{"t":"table","caption":"PII handling by stage","head":["Stage","Option","What to know"],"rows":[["At ingest","Azure AI Language PII: text PII (synchronous strings), conversation PII (asynchronous) and document PII (asynchronous, native `.pdf`, `.docx`, `.txt`, returns a redacted file and JSON metadata) [[r18]]","Redaction can remove the signal needed to answer, so decide what must stay linkable and keep it under access control"],["At query time","Presidio: analyzer, anonymizer, image redactor and structured data module. Recognizers combine NER, regex, rule logic with checksums and context, and can call external detectors such as Azure AI Language [[r17]]","The docs warn there is no guarantee it finds all PII, so add custom recognizers for your formats"],["On output","Foundry PII filter: scans model output only, entity types follow Azure Language categories, annotate or annotate and block (the whole output) [[r8]]","Preview. Does not cover prompts, retrieved chunks or logs"],["In logs and traces","Redact before storing, with the same detector you trust elsewhere","The output filter never sees what you log from the input side"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes of self-run guards","items":[{"symptom":"Every answer is blocked after swapping to a reasoning model.","cause":"`self check facts` returned 0.0 because the model used its `max_tokens` budget before answering, and the rail fails closed.","fix":"Raise the token budget or use a non-reasoning model for the check, and alert on a sudden block spike."},{"symptom":"Legitimate regulated advice is blocked.","cause":"A broad hazard category such as Specialized Advice matches normal answers.","fix":"Rewrite the category text for your domain, measure false positives per category and keep a human review path."},{"symptom":"A local national ID format slips through.","cause":"No detector guarantees full PII coverage, and the format has no recognizer.","fix":"Add a custom recognizer with a checksum, build a test set of your formats and track misses."},{"symptom":"The output is clean and PII still shows up in logs.","cause":"The output filter does not see prompts, chunks or log writes.","fix":"Redact at ingest and before logging, and test the log path."},{"symptom":"Behaviour changes after a framework upgrade.","cause":"Rails, prompts and validators change between versions. NeMo's docs list releases from v0.22.0 to v0.24.1 and a Latest build.","fix":"Pin versions and re-run your guard evaluation set on every upgrade."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Presidio moved","md":"Presidio now lives under the community-owned Data Privacy Stack, with docs at presidio.dataprivacystack.org and source at data-privacy-stack/presidio. Old Microsoft URLs redirect, so update bookmarks and dependency notes. Checked 2026-10-13."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-l4-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"A regulated assistant must keep text on its own servers, enforce custom policy categories and serve several languages. Which stack fits best?","options":["Self-hosted Llama Guard with custom category text, run through NeMo Guardrails","Foundry guardrails with Custom categories (standard) to carry the custom policy","Guardrails AI validators that call a hosted detection API for every check","An instruction in the system prompt that lists the policy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Open weights keep text on your servers, rewritten category text carries the custom taxonomy, and NeMo supplies the flow control. You still have to test each language. Custom categories (standard) is retired or retiring and managed, hosted-API validators send text out, and a prompt instruction is not an enforced guard."},{"id":"w06-l4-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"After switching the generator to a reasoning model, every answer is blocked. The log shows the following. What is the cause?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"rail: self check facts\nmodel: reasoning model, max_tokens=200\nscore: 0.0\naction: blocked\nretrieved chunks: 5"},"options":["The knowledge base lacks the chunks needed, so the rail scored the answer zero","A retrieval rail removed every chunk before generation began, leaving nothing to score","The reasoning model spent its `max_tokens` budget, so the rail failed closed","A Llama Guard S7 Privacy label fired on every answer, which the rail blocks"],"answer":2,"explain":"NeMo's fact-check rail returns 0.0 and blocks when a reasoning model uses its whole token budget thinking, which fails closed. The log still shows five retrieved chunks, so retrieval and the chunks are fine, and nothing in it shows a Llama Guard label."},{"id":"w06-l4-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","difficulty":2,"q":"Select every true statement about PII controls.","options":["Presidio's documentation warns there is no guarantee that it finds all PII","The Foundry PII filter scans model output only and is a preview feature","The Foundry PII filter also scans user prompts and retrieved chunks","Azure AI Language document PII is asynchronous and accepts native .pdf, .docx and .txt files"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The first, second and fourth statements match the docs. The output filter does not scan prompts or chunks, which is why redaction at ingest and before logging is separate work."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r13","r14","r15","r16","r17","r18","r8","r11","r28","r29"],"takeaways":["Data location and policy shape decide most platform versus self-hosted choices","Llama Guard lets you rewrite category text, and NeMo gives you flow control with five rail types","No PII detector guarantees full coverage, so test your own formats and cover logs","Pin guard framework versions and re-run your evaluation set after upgrades"]},{"id":"w06-l5","order":5,"title":"Price the false alarms and test the guards","kind":"problem","minutes":16,"brief":"A guard that blocks too much is a quiet outage, and one that blocks too little is a headline. How do you put a price on each error and test a guard before users meet it?","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Every guard is a classifier with two error rates","level":2,"id":"every-guard-is-a-classifier-with-two-error-rates"},{"t":"formula","title":"Why most blocks are false alarms","expr":"precision = (recall x base rate) / (recall x base rate + FPR x (1 - base rate))","vars":[{"k":"recall","v":"share of harmful requests the guard catches"},{"k":"base rate","v":"share of all traffic that is harmful"},{"k":"FPR","v":"false positive rate, the share of benign requests the guard blocks"}],"example":"With a 0.5% base rate, 95% recall and a 2% false positive rate, precision is 0.00475 / (0.00475 + 0.0199) = **0.193**. About four in five blocks hit a benign request, even though the guard looks excellent."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Harmful requests","value":"500","note":"Per 100,000 requests at a 0.5% base rate. Illustrative arithmetic, not vendor data."},{"label":"Harm caught","value":"475","note":"95% recall."},{"label":"Benign requests blocked","value":"1,990","note":"2% of 99,500 benign requests."},{"label":"Share of blocks that are real","value":"19%","note":"475 of 2,465 blocks."}]},{"t":"compare","title":"What a guard does when it fires","intro":"The action changes what a false positive costs. Foundry offers annotate and annotate and block for models, and agents support annotate and block only (preview) [[r1]].","options":[{"name":"Block","summary":"Stop the request or the answer.","pros":["Strongest protection","Simple to explain"],"cons":["Every false positive is a failed request","Block cannot tell the user what to change"],"bestWhen":"Harm is severe and irreversible, such as unsafe tool actions.","avoidWhen":"The guard is new or its precision is unmeasured."},{"name":"Annotate (shadow mode)","summary":"Record the verdict and let the content through.","pros":["Measures real block rates and false positives with no user impact","Builds the labelled set you need"],"cons":["Harm gets through while you learn","Annotations nobody reads change nothing"],"bestWhen":"Launching or tuning a guard.","avoidWhen":"Permanent use on a severe-harm surface."},{"name":"Repair","summary":"Redact, rewrite or correct instead of refusing, for example PII redaction or groundedness correction (preview).","pros":["Keeps most of the user's help","Lowers the cost of a false positive"],"cons":["A repair can introduce its own errors","Needs a second check on the repaired text"],"bestWhen":"A small part of the content is the problem.","avoidWhen":"Any residue of the content is unacceptable."},{"name":"Escalate to a human","summary":"Hold the item for review or sample it afterwards.","pros":["Handles borderline cases","Feeds labels back to the evaluation set"],"cons":["Adds delay and a staffing cost","Does not scale to every request"],"bestWhen":"High stakes and low volume.","avoidWhen":"Real-time chat at scale."}]},{"t":"flow","title":"Evaluate a guard before users meet it","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Label","md":"Build sets for benign, harmful, borderline and adversarial inputs, per language.","tag":"data"},{"title":"Measure","md":"Recall and false positive rate per category and language, plus latency p50 and p95.","tag":"offline"},{"title":"Shadow","md":"Annotate only on live traffic and compare verdicts with your labels.","tag":"live"},{"title":"Review","md":"Sample blocks and misses with humans.","tag":"people"},{"title":"Set","md":"Choose thresholds per category and surface.","tag":"tune"},{"title":"Canary","md":"Enable blocking for a small share, then widen with alerts.","tag":"rollout"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"What to measure and alert on","head":["Metric","Why it matters","Alert when"],"rows":[["Recall per category and language on a red-team set","Shows what slips through","It falls below the agreed floor after any change"],["False positive rate on benign traffic","The cost users feel","It exceeds budget or differs by language"],["Block rate over time","Detects drift, including provider model updates","It moves outside an agreed band overnight"],["Latency p50 and p95 per guard","Parallel guards add the slowest one","A guard becomes the slowest stage"],["Guard timeout and error rate","Reveals fail-open and fail-closed paths","It rises and stays up"],["Override or appeal rate","Shows false positives users notice","It rises"]],"highlightCol":2},{"t":"failure","title":"Guard evaluation failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The block rate doubles overnight and nothing changed on your side.","cause":"A classifier or service model update moved its decision boundary.","fix":"Alert on block rate, re-run the labelled set, compare recall and false positives with the last run, and keep a rollback path."},{"symptom":"Rephrased or encoded attacks get through.","cause":"The test set holds only plain attacks. Prompt Shields lists encoding attacks and role-play among its subtypes [[r3]].","fix":"Add encodings, role-play, conversation mockups and document-borne variants to the adversarial set."},{"symptom":"The guard scores well and users in another language complain.","cause":"Evaluation ran in English only, and several features were tested on English only [[r10]].","fix":"Slice every metric by language and keep a per-language floor."},{"symptom":"Medical or legal vocabulary is blocked.","cause":"One threshold across all surfaces.","fix":"Set thresholds per category and surface, review a sample of blocks, and consider custom categories."},{"symptom":"Annotations pile up and nobody reads them.","cause":"Shadow mode has no owner or review cadence.","fix":"Assign an owner, sample weekly and set an exit date for shadow mode."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-l5-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":3,"q":"Harmful traffic is 1% of requests. A different guard has 90% recall and a 3% false positive rate. What share of its blocks are real harm (precision)?","answer":0.233,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"fraction","hint":"0.90 x 0.01 divided by (0.90 x 0.01 + 0.03 x 0.99)","explain":"True blocks are 0.90 x 0.01 = 0.009 of traffic and false blocks are 0.03 x 0.99 = 0.0297, so precision is 0.009 / 0.0387 = 0.233. Recall alone says nothing about the user-visible cost, because benign traffic is almost all of the traffic."},{"id":"w06-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"The block rate for one harm category doubled overnight. You deployed nothing, and complaints about false positives are rising. What do you do first?","options":["Disable the guard until the weekend, then review the complaints","Re-run the labelled set and compare with the last run, checking for a provider update","Raise every threshold to the highest level so that the complaints stop and the block rate settles","Replace the classifier with an LLM judge and relaunch it as a new project"],"answer":1,"explain":"A sudden shift with no deployment points to a classifier or service update, and only a re-run of your labelled set separates a drift in the guard from a change in traffic. Disabling the guard removes protection, raising every threshold hides real harm, and swapping in a judge is a redesign, not a diagnosis."},{"id":"w06-l5-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"q":"Put the steps for evaluating and rolling out a guard in order.","items":["Label benign, harmful, borderline and adversarial sets per language","Measure recall, false positives and latency offline","Run in shadow mode on live traffic","Review a sample of blocks and misses with people","Set thresholds per category and surface","Enable blocking for a canary share"],"explain":"Offline measurement comes before live exposure, and shadow mode comes before blocking so that no user pays for tuning. Thresholds are set after the review, because the review shows where they are wrong, and the canary ends the sequence."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r3","r10","r4","r26","r27"],"takeaways":["At low base rates most blocks hit benign requests, even for a good guard","Shadow mode measures false positives before anyone is blocked","Slice metrics by category and language, and alert on block rate drift","Choose the action by the cost of a false positive: block, annotate, repair or escalate"]},{"id":"w06-l6","order":6,"title":"Boss: guard a regulated assistant","kind":"boss","minutes":18,"brief":"An interviewer asks you to design the guardrail stack for an assistant that answers customers' questions about their own regulated account data, using RAG and a few tools. No dials are given.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Your four minutes","level":2,"id":"your-four-minutes"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you are handed","known":["Customers ask about their own account data, and some answers drive decisions","The assistant retrieves documents and can call a few tools","The interviewer will probe latency, streaming, languages and what happens when a guard fails"],"ask":["Which rules apply, and what is the worst outcome: harm, disclosure or a wrong figure?","Which languages, what latency budget and must answers stream?","Which tools have side effects, and where may text go?","How are permissions enforced in retrieval?"],"tension":"More guards mean fewer incidents and more latency, more false blocks and more things to operate."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w06-l6-stack","title":"A defensible default guard stack","alt":"The user input passes shields and a PII check, retrieval applies an access filter, retrieved chunks pass a document attack scan, the model answers with citations, tool calls pass an allow list, and the output passes groundedness, harm and PII checks before an answer or an abstention.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User] --> IG[Input shields and PII check]\n  IG --> RT[Retrieve with access filter]\n  RT --> RG[Document attack scan]\n  RG --> M[Model with citations]\n  M --> TG[Tool allow list]\n  M --> OG[Groundedness, harm, PII]\n  OG --> A[Answer or abstain]","caption":"A starting point. Every box has a dial that can remove, replace or move it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-l6-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":3,"q":"The product team insists on token streaming for the chat UI, and the risk tier stays high. Which change is most defensible?","options":["Switch every output to asynchronous filter mode and rely on annotations","Remove the groundedness check and keep only the input filters","Stream directly and retract the answer if the claim check fails later","Use buffered streaming and show account figures only after the claim check passes"],"answer":3,"explain":"Buffered streaming filters each chunk before it is shown, and holding account-specific claims until they are checked keeps wrong figures off the screen. Asynchronous filter mode lets violating text appear before the stop signal, removing the claim check removes the grounding control, and retracting a shown figure after the fact is already a disclosure or a wrong answer."},{"id":"w06-l6-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"q":"What is the most serious problem in this guard orchestration for a regulated assistant?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"def answer(q, user):\n    if input_guard(q) == \"block\":\n        return REFUSAL\n    chunks = retrieve(q, user)\n    draft = llm(q, chunks)\n    try:\n        verdict = groundedness_check(draft, chunks, timeout=0.5)\n    except TimeoutError:\n        verdict = \"grounded\"\n    return draft if verdict == \"grounded\" else ABSTAIN"},"options":["A timeout is treated as grounded, so the guard fails open","The input guard should run after retrieval, not before it","ABSTAIN should be returned inside the try block, not outside it","The question must reach the guard before the chunks do"],"answer":0,"explain":"When the check times out the code lets the draft through, so an outage or a slow dependency removes the control exactly when it is stressed. A regulated assistant should fail closed or degrade to an abstention. Running the input guard first is correct, and the position of ABSTAIN and the argument order do not change the failure."},{"id":"w06-l6-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","difficulty":3,"q":"Select every control that belongs in this design.","options":["Pass the user's identity so retrieval applies document permissions before generation","Scan retrieved chunks and tool responses for document attacks","Allow-list tools and validate their arguments in code","Rely on Prompt Shields alone because it blocks all prompt injection"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Permissions, document-attack scanning and deterministic tool controls each cover a path a content filter cannot. Prompt Shields is a probabilistic detector, so treating it as a complete defence leaves injection and exfiltration paths open."}],"defend":{"prompt":"Design the guardrail stack for an assistant that answers customers' questions about their own regulated account data. It uses RAG and can call a few tools. Walk me through your design.","timebox":"4 min","clarify":["Which regulations or internal policies apply, and what is the worst outcome: harm, disclosure or a wrong figure?","Which languages must be supported?","What is the latency budget, and must answers stream to the user?","Which tools exist and which of them have side effects?","May text go to managed services, or only to our own servers?","How are permissions modelled, and where are they enforced in retrieval?"],"options":[{"name":"Platform guardrails plus deterministic checks","when":"Managed services are acceptable and the harms are the standard categories.","tradeoff":"Several features are preview or English-tested, and a custom policy is awkward."},{"name":"Platform plus a claim-level check and tool controls","when":"Risk is high and the content is in tested languages.","tradeoff":"Adds latency and conflicts with token streaming, and agent tool controls are preview."},{"name":"Self-hosted stack: NeMo Guardrails, Llama Guard, Presidio","when":"Text must stay on our servers or the policy is custom.","tradeoff":"You operate it and tune false positives yourself."},{"name":"LLM judge only","when":"A quick pilot with low stakes.","tradeoff":"Slow, non-deterministic, and blind to tool side effects."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** I would ask which rules apply and what the worst outcome is, which languages, the latency budget and whether answers must stream, which tools have side effects, whether text may leave our servers and how permissions are enforced in retrieval."},{"t":"p","md":"**2. List the options.** Platform guardrails with deterministic checks is the boring baseline. Adding a claim-level check and tool controls raises coverage and latency. A self-hosted stack keeps text in-house. An LLM judge alone is quick and weak."},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Pick for the stated dials.** For regulated account data I would layer, and fail closed. Deterministic checks first: schemas, allow lists, size caps and output format. Prompt Shields and a PII check on the input, run in parallel with retrieval. Permission filtering inside retrieval, then a document attack scan on retrieved chunks and tool responses. Tools behind an allow list with validated arguments. Generation with citations, then a claim-level check, using Azure groundedness detection only if the content is English and a preview feature is acceptable, otherwise an NLI model or judge. Harm and PII filters on output. Buffered streaming, or the whole answer after checks. Any guard timeout becomes an abstention or an escalation."},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Failure modes and detection.**"},{"t":"table","head":["Failure","How I would detect it"],"rows":[["Injection through a retrieved page or tool result","Planted-instruction tests in CI, document attack detections per source"],["Guard timeout lets answers through","Timeout and error rate per guard, with an alert, and a test that forces a timeout"],["Over-blocking of legitimate account questions","False positive rate per category and language, override rate, sampled review"],["Citation that does not support the claim","Support rate from a sampled entailment check"],["Non-English attack bypass","Per-language red-team recall with a floor"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** Offline: recall per category and language on a red-team set, false positive rate on benign traffic, groundedness and citation support. Shadow mode on live traffic first, then a canary with blocking, with alerts on block rate, guard latency p95 and timeout rate."},{"t":"p","md":"**What would flip the choice.**"},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Token streaming is mandatory: keep buffered streaming and hold account figures until checked, never asynchronous filter for this risk tier.","Text may not leave our network: move to the self-hosted stack with custom categories, and budget for tuning.","Content is mostly non-English: lean on a tunable judge or NLI model per language, because several platform features were tested on English only.","Latency is tight: run independent guards in parallel and move the claim check to high-risk answers only.","A preview feature changes or retires: keep a deterministic or self-hosted fallback for that control."]}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about rules, worst outcome, languages, latency, data location and tools before naming components","w":3},{"pt":"Places guards at input, retrieval, tool and output, not only at input","w":3},{"pt":"Separates answerability, groundedness and citation validity","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses fail-closed behaviour and abstention for the regulated setting","w":2},{"pt":"States feature status (GA, preview, English-only) and what flips the choice","w":2},{"pt":"Handles latency with parallel guards and states the streaming trade-off","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure modes with a detection signal for each","w":2},{"pt":"Gives an evaluation and rollout plan with shadow mode and per-language slices","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"Product demands token streaming. What changes?","a":"Keep buffered streaming so each chunk is filtered before display, and hold account-specific claims until the claim check passes. I would not use the asynchronous filter here, because violating text can show before the stop signal."},{"q":"Legal says text may not leave our network.","a":"Move to the self-hosted stack: Llama Guard with rewritten category text, NeMo Guardrails for flow control, Presidio for PII, and a self-hosted NLI or judge for claims. I would budget for tuning false positives and for operating the models."},{"q":"How do you know the guards are not blocking good answers?","a":"Shadow mode first, a labelled benign set, false positive rate per category and language, the override rate and sampled review. At low base rates most blocks can be false alarms even for a good guard, so precision is the number to watch."}],"redFlags":["Treats a content filter or Prompt Shields as a complete defence against injection","Guards only the user input","Lets guard timeouts pass requests through","Names a vendor feature without its status or language limits"]},"refs":["r1","r3","r4","r9","r11","r13","r16","r17"],"takeaways":["Layer guards across input, retrieval, tools and output, and fail closed","Streaming and claim-level checks pull in opposite directions, so state which wins","Name each feature's status and language limits, and keep a fallback for preview controls","Measure false positives and per-language recall, in shadow mode before blocking"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w06-t01","order":1,"group":"Placement","title":"Guardrail placement: input, retrieval, output, tool","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A guard only sees what passes through its position. Input, retrieved content, tool calls and output each carry different risks, so placement decides what a guard can catch and what it costs in latency.","useIf":"You are deciding where to put checks around a RAG or agent request and how much latency each may add.","avoidIf":"You treat guardrails as one filter on the final answer: that misses untrusted documents, tool side effects and early leaks.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Foundry guardrails define four **intervention points**: user input, tool call (preview, agents only), tool response (preview, agents only) and output [[r1]]. NeMo Guardrails names five rail types: input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output, where retrieval rails filter chunks and execution rails check tool calls [[r13]]. Different names, same idea: each point sees different data."},{"t":"p","md":"**Input guards** catch attacks and unwanted topics before the model spends tokens. **Retrieval guards** treat retrieved chunks as untrusted: a document can carry an instruction that the user never typed, which Prompt Shields calls a document (indirect) attack [[r3]]. **Tool guards** run before a side effect happens, so they must sit in front of execution, not behind the answer. **Output guards** check what the user will see. Microsoft's defence-in-depth guidance combines prompt hardening, Spotlighting, Prompt Shields and deterministic blocking of exfiltration paths, not one filter [[r11]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Latency.** No vendor in the sources publishes guard latency, so measure it. As an author estimate, a classifier call adds tens to a few hundred milliseconds, and an LLM judge adds a full model call of one to several seconds. Sequential guards add up. Independent guards run in parallel add only the slowest. Streaming changes the output trade-off: the default buffers and filters chunks, while the asynchronous filter streams token by token and guarantees the stop signal only within about 1,000 characters [[r9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w06-t01-d1","title":"Where each guard stands in a RAG request","alt":"A user prompt passes an input guard, retrieval runs, a chunk guard checks retrieved content, the model runs, then a tool guard checks proposed tool calls and an output guard checks the answer before it reaches the user.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User] --> G1[Input guard]\n  G1 --> R[Retrieve]\n  R --> G2[Chunk guard]\n  G2 --> M[Model]\n  M --> G3[Tool guard]\n  M --> G4[Output guard]\n  G4 --> A[Answer]"},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Retrieval can fail open","md":"Retrieve calls without a user identity header return unfiltered results from indexed sources [[tr2]]. A guard placed after retrieval cannot restore a permission check that was skipped before it."}],"whereToUse":["Designing the guard stack for a RAG assistant before choosing products","Reviewing an agent that can send email, write records or call external APIs","Deciding which checks run in parallel with retrieval to protect the latency budget","Explaining to security reviewers what each guard can and cannot see","Mapping Foundry intervention points or NeMo rail types onto your own pipeline"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Put an input guard in front of every model call that accepts user text","Put a chunk guard on retrieved or tool-returned content when sources are not fully trusted","Put a tool guard before any call with a side effect","Put an output guard where harm comes from what the user reads"],"avoid":["Avoid a single output-only guard for agents: tool side effects happen before the answer","Avoid running every guard sequentially when they are independent","Avoid assuming the model-level guardrail covers tool calls on an agent"],"flips":["Latency budget is tight: run input guards in parallel with retrieval and reserve LLM judges for high-risk routes","Sources are fully trusted and curated: the chunk guard can be lighter or sampled","Tools are read-only: tool-call guards matter less, tool-response scanning matters more"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Placement","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Input guard","Stops attacks and off-policy topics early","Risk is inside retrieved documents","Adds latency to every request unless run in parallel"],["Retrieval or chunk guard","Untrusted or user-supplied documents","Corpus is curated and small","More calls per request, false positives on benign text"],["Tool-call guard","Tools write, send or delete","Tools are read-only","Must sit before execution, preview for Foundry agents"],["Output guard","Harm comes from what is shown","A side effect already happened","Late: cost of generation is spent, streaming leaks if async"],["Post-hoc audit","Low-risk analytics, review loops","Harm is immediate","Detects after the fact only"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"An agent sends a harmful email although the final answer passed the output guard","cause":"No guard stood in front of the tool call","fix":"Add tool-call controls or a deterministic allow list before execution"},{"symptom":"Added guards double the response time","cause":"Independent checks were chained in sequence","fix":"Run independent guards in parallel and tier the expensive ones"},{"symptom":"A planted instruction in a PDF steers the answer","cause":"Only user input was scanned, not retrieved chunks","fix":"Scan chunks and tool responses for document attacks and reduce tool privileges"},{"symptom":"Tool calls are unscanned after an agent guardrail was created","cause":"An agent's guardrail fully overrides its model's, and it had no tool controls","fix":"Include tool call and tool response controls in the agent guardrail"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t01-s1","title":"Read-only assistant over uploaded documents","situation":"Users upload PDFs and ask questions. The assistant has no tools and answers in under three seconds. Uploaded files come from outside the company.","constraints":["No tools","Untrusted documents","3 second latency budget","Moderate harm if the answer is steered"],"unknowns":["Can the answer reach other users?","Is streaming used?"],"question":"Which guards do you place?","options":[{"name":"Input guard in parallel with retrieval, a chunk guard for document attacks, and an output guard","verdict":"best","why":"The risk sits in the documents, so chunk scanning is essential, and parallel input checks protect the budget.","tradeoff":"More guard calls and a small false positive rate on benign text."},{"name":"Output guard only","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and catches visible harm.","tradeoff":"A steered but plausible answer passes, and the cost of generation is already spent."},{"name":"Input guard only","verdict":"poor","why":"The user prompt is clean while the attack lives in the document.","tradeoff":"Right for chat with no retrieval and no documents."}],"flips":[{"if":"Documents come only from a curated, trusted library","then":"Sample chunk scanning and keep input and output guards."},{"if":"Latency budget shrinks below one second","then":"Keep only deterministic checks inline and move model-based guards to async review."}],"takeaway":"Guard the place where the untrusted content enters, not only the place where the user types."},{"id":"w06-t01-s2","title":"Agent that can send email","situation":"An agent reads a mailbox through a tool and can send replies. It streams answers. A mistaken or injected send cannot be undone.","constraints":["Irreversible side effect","Tool responses contain external text","Streaming UI","Foundry Agent Service"],"unknowns":["Which recipients are allowed?","Is human approval available for sends?"],"question":"Where must the strongest guard stand?","options":[{"name":"Before the send: tool-call guard plus a deterministic allow list and approval, with tool-response scanning on the mailbox read","verdict":"best","why":"The harm happens at execution, so the check has to stand in front of it, and mailbox text is an indirect attack path.","tradeoff":"Tool-call and tool-response controls are preview, and approvals add friction."},{"name":"Input and output guards, plus human review of sent mail afterwards","verdict":"good","why":"Catches obvious problems and gives an audit trail.","tradeoff":"Review happens after an irreversible send."},{"name":"Output guard only","verdict":"poor","why":"The email is already sent when the final answer is checked.","tradeoff":"Right for text-only assistants with no side effects."}],"flips":[{"if":"Sends are reversible drafts","then":"Output and audit guards are enough."},{"if":"Preview controls are not allowed","then":"Use deterministic allow lists and approvals in your own code."}],"takeaway":"Put the strongest guard in front of the action that cannot be undone."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"An agent's final answers pass the output guard, yet it sent a bad email. What is the most likely gap?","options":["The embedding model was too small","The reranker was disabled","The index was stale","No guard stood in front of the tool call"],"answer":3,"explain":"Side effects occur before the answer is produced, so a tool-call guard or deterministic control must stand in front of execution. Embedding size, reranking and staleness do not explain a bad send.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w06-t01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are valid ways to protect a tight latency budget?","options":["Run independent input guards in parallel with retrieval","Chain every guard in sequence","Reserve LLM judges for high-risk routes","Use deterministic checks inline for the cheapest filters"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Parallelism, tiering and cheap deterministic checks save time. Sequential chains add the sum of their latencies."},{"id":"w06-t01-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each NeMo Guardrails rail type to its job.","pairs":[{"l":"Input rail","r":"Checks the user message before the model"},{"l":"Retrieval rail","r":"Filters retrieved chunks"},{"l":"Execution rail","r":"Checks tool calls"},{"l":"Output rail","r":"Checks the generated answer"}],"explain":"Each rail type sees a different part of the request. NeMo also has a dialog rail type that governs conversation flow.","ref":"r13"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Name the four Foundry guardrail intervention points.","back":"User input, tool call (preview, agents only), tool response (preview, agents only) and output."},{"front":"Why scan retrieved chunks and tool responses?","back":"They are untrusted text that can carry instructions the user never typed, so input guards alone miss document attacks."},{"front":"How do parallel guards affect latency?","back":"Independent parallel guards add roughly the slowest one. Sequential guards add the sum."}],"related":["w06-t02","w06-t05","w06-t07","w06-t10","w06-l1","w07-t02"],"refs":["r1","r3","r9","r11","r13","tr2"]},{"id":"w06-t02","order":2,"group":"Grounding","title":"Groundedness and faithfulness checks","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A groundedness check asks whether every claim in an answer is supported by the evidence the model was given. It is a separate question from whether the answer is true, relevant or cited.","useIf":"Wrong answers are costly and you can afford a second pass over the answer and its evidence.","avoidIf":"Latency is tight and the domain is low risk: a prompt that permits \"I don't know\" and a sampled offline check may be enough.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Faithfulness** (groundedness) means each claim is entailed by the retrieved context. Natural language inference (NLI) models test this: given the evidence as premise and a claim as hypothesis, predict entailment, neutral or contradiction. SummaC applies NLI at the sentence level and aggregates the scores, which fixes a granularity mismatch between NLI training data and long documents [[r19]]. TRUE meta-evaluates factual-consistency metrics across tasks, and AlignScore is a unified alignment function trained on many tasks [[r20]] [[r21]]. A prompted LLM judge can do the same job at the price of a model call."},{"t":"p","md":"**Azure AI Content Safety groundedness detection** is preview. It takes the answer and grounding sources, supports the domains `Medical` and `Generic` and the tasks `Summarization` and `QnA`, and is English only. Non-reasoning mode returns a fast grounded or ungrounded verdict, reasoning mode explains the ungrounded segments (use it while developing), and optional correction returns repaired text. Limits are 55,000 characters of grounding sources, 7,500 characters of text plus query, and a query of at least 3 words. In Foundry guardrails it applies to models, not agents [[r4]] [[r10]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**NeMo Guardrails** offers an output rail, `self check facts`, that uses `$relevant_chunks` as evidence and returns a 0.0 to 1.0 score. Quality depends on the LLM following the `self_check_facts` prompt, and if a reasoning model spends its `max_tokens` budget before answering, the rail fails closed with a score of 0.0 and the response is blocked [[r14]]. For very long documents, Anthropic's guidance is to ask for word-for-word quotes first when the document exceeds 20k tokens, and to give the model explicit permission to say it does not know [[r23]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"A claim-level check","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Split","md":"Break the answer into atomic claims.","tag":"claims"},{"title":"Pair","md":"Match each claim with the evidence spans it cites or retrieves.","tag":"evidence"},{"title":"Score","md":"Run NLI or a judge per claim, not per answer.","tag":"entailment"},{"title":"Compare","md":"Use a threshold calibrated on a labelled set.","tag":"threshold"},{"title":"Act","md":"Pass, repair the claim, abstain or block.","tag":"action"}]}],"whereToUse":["RAG answers in finance, health, legal or policy settings where unsupported text causes harm","Summarisation pipelines where the source text is the only allowed evidence","Offline evaluation sets that measure hallucination rate before launch","Output rails that block or repair an answer before the user sees it","Long-document tasks that use quote-first prompts and verify quotes by string match"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use claim-level NLI or a judge when a single invented sentence is costly","Use Azure groundedness detection for English content when preview status is acceptable and sources fit the limits","Use quote-first prompting for documents over about 20k tokens","Calibrate thresholds on your own labelled examples, in each language you serve"],"avoid":["Avoid answer-level checks that let one fabricated sentence hide among good ones","Avoid using a groundedness score as proof of correctness: the evidence itself can be wrong","Avoid copying a threshold from another corpus or model"],"flips":["Traffic is not English: use a judge or NLI model you evaluated per language instead of the English-only API","Latency budget cannot carry a second pass: sample offline and rely on abstention prompts online","Grounding set exceeds 55,000 characters: split it, use quote-first or choose another checker"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Method","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Azure groundedness detection","English, short sources, fast verdict wanted","Other languages or sources over 55,000 characters","Preview, English only, models not agents"],["NLI model per claim (SummaC, AlignScore style)","You can host a model and need claim-level scores","Domain jargon unseen by the model","Hosting, calibration, sentence splitting"],["LLM judge","Any language, flexible rubric","Tight latency or cost","An extra model call, judge bias and leniency"],["Quote-then-answer","Long documents, verifiable quotes","Answers need synthesis across many passages","Longer outputs, string-match verification step"],["Prompt-only (\"say I don't know\")","Low risk, minimal latency","Wrong answers are costly","No measurement, relies on model compliance"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A fluent answer passes the check but contains one invented figure","cause":"The check scored the whole answer, not each claim","fix":"Split into claims and check each against its evidence"},{"symptom":"The judge approves claims that the context does not contain","cause":"The judge used its own knowledge, or saw the question without isolated evidence","fix":"Give only the evidence, ask for entailment per claim, and test with known-bad answers"},{"symptom":"Every answer is blocked after switching to a reasoning model","cause":"The NeMo rail fails closed when the model spends its `max_tokens` before answering","fix":"Raise the budget or use a non-reasoning model for the rail"},{"symptom":"The groundedness API rejects or mis-scores some requests","cause":"Inputs exceed the 55,000 or 7,500 character limits, the query is under 3 words, or the text is not English","fix":"Segment inputs, enforce limits and route other languages elsewhere"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t02-s1","title":"Multilingual support answers","situation":"A support assistant answers in English, German and Japanese from a knowledge base. An unsupported claim causes a wrong refund promise. Another second or two of latency is acceptable.","constraints":["Several languages","Moderate harm per error","1 to 2 extra seconds allowed","A labelled test set per language can be built"],"unknowns":["What is the mix of languages in traffic?","Is preview status acceptable?"],"question":"How do you check groundedness?","options":[{"name":"Per-claim LLM judge or NLI model evaluated on each language, with calibrated thresholds","verdict":"best","why":"It works in all served languages and you can measure it on labelled examples.","tradeoff":"Extra model call and the work of building per-language test sets."},{"name":"Azure groundedness detection for English traffic and a judge for the rest","verdict":"good","why":"Uses the managed API where it is supported.","tradeoff":"Two code paths and a preview dependency."},{"name":"Azure groundedness detection for every language","verdict":"poor","why":"It is English only, so non-English checks are unreliable.","tradeoff":"Right when all traffic is English."}],"flips":[{"if":"Traffic is 99 percent English","then":"The managed API for English plus sampled review for the rest may be enough."},{"if":"No labelled data exists yet","then":"Start in shadow mode and build the set from logged cases."}],"takeaway":"Language support decides which checker you may trust."},{"id":"w06-t02-s2","title":"Answers over very long contracts","situation":"Users ask about clauses in contracts that run past 20,000 tokens of evidence. Answers must be verifiable by a lawyer in seconds. English only.","constraints":["Evidence beyond 20k tokens","Verifiable quotes needed","Accuracy outranks speed","English"],"unknowns":["Can answers be built from a few clauses?","How are quotes displayed to the reviewer?"],"question":"Which design fits?","options":[{"name":"Quote-then-answer, verify each quote by string match, then check claims against the quotes","verdict":"best","why":"Quotes are cheap to verify exactly, and the second check then runs on a short, verified evidence set.","tradeoff":"Longer outputs and two stages."},{"name":"Claim-level NLI over the full retrieved context","verdict":"good","why":"Works without changing the answer format.","tradeoff":"Long inputs weaken NLI and increase cost."},{"name":"Azure groundedness detection over the whole contract","verdict":"poor","why":"Its 55,000 character cap on grounding sources is roughly 13,750 tokens by the 4 characters per token rule of thumb, below the evidence size.","tradeoff":"Right for short grounding sets."}],"flips":[{"if":"Evidence is under 55,000 characters and preview is acceptable","then":"The managed API is a quick second check."},{"if":"Answers must synthesise across many clauses","then":"Quote-first gets harder, so rely on claim-level checks with clause-level evidence."}],"takeaway":"Shrink the evidence before checking it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t02-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Azure groundedness detection accepts 55,000 characters of grounding sources. Using about 4 characters per token, roughly how many tokens is that?","answer":13750,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Divide characters by 4.","explain":"55,000 divided by 4 is 13,750 tokens, an estimate from a rule of thumb. It is below the 20k token threshold at which quote-first prompting is advised, so long documents need another approach.","ref":"r4"},{"id":"w06-t02-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does groundedness measure?","options":["Whether each claim is supported by the supplied evidence","Whether the answer is true in the real world","Whether the answer is polite","Whether the retrieval was fast"],"answer":0,"explain":"Groundedness is about support by the given evidence. A grounded answer can still be wrong if the evidence is wrong, and politeness and speed are unrelated.","ref":"r19"},{"id":"w06-t02-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which are documented limits or behaviours of Azure groundedness detection?","options":["English only","Grounding sources up to 55,000 characters","Applies to agents in Foundry guardrails","Reasoning mode explains ungrounded segments"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"It is English only, accepts 55,000 characters of sources and has a reasoning mode for explanations. In Foundry guardrails it applies to models, not agents.","ref":"r4"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Groundedness versus correctness?","back":"Groundedness asks whether claims are supported by the supplied evidence. An answer can be grounded and still wrong if the evidence is wrong."},{"front":"Azure groundedness detection limits?","back":"Preview, English only. Grounding sources 55,000 characters, text plus query 7,500 characters, query at least 3 words. Models, not agents."},{"front":"Why check at claim level?","back":"A whole-answer score can hide one invented sentence. Per-claim entailment catches it (SummaC splits at sentence level and aggregates)."}],"related":["w06-t03","w06-t06","w06-t10","w06-l2","w14-t09"],"refs":["r4","r10","r14","r19","r20","r21","r23"]},{"id":"w06-t03","order":3,"group":"Grounding","title":"Citation enforcement, abstention and answerability","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Answerability decides whether the evidence can answer at all, citation enforcement forces each claim to point at a source span, and abstention is the safe response when it cannot.","useIf":"A confident wrong answer costs more than \"I could not find that\", and users or auditors need to trace claims to sources.","avoidIf":"The assistant is a creative or brainstorming tool where no source of truth exists.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Three checks sound alike and differ. **Answerability** asks before generation whether the retrieved evidence is sufficient. **Citation validity** asks after generation whether each citation points at a real span. **Support** asks whether that span entails the claim. A citation proves **provenance**, not **entailment**, so you still need a check such as NLI or quote verification. ALCE is a benchmark for answers with citations, and AIS (Attributable to Identified Sources) is an annotation framework for whether output is supported by a source [[r22]] [[r23]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**API-native citations** help: Anthropic's Citations feature is enabled with `citations` on `document` or `search_result` blocks, returns types such as `char_location`, `page_location` and `content_block_location`, does not count `cited_text` toward output tokens and works with prompt caching, token counting and batch. It is incompatible with structured outputs: enabling both returns HTTP 400 [[r24]]. **Prompt-based** enforcement asks for word-for-word quotes first on long documents and gives explicit permission to say \"I don't know\" [[r23]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Abstention thresholds** need care. Azure's semantic reranker score runs from 0 to 4, and it only reorders the top 50 candidates, so poor recall before reranking cannot be fixed by a threshold [[tr1]]. Scores drift across model updates, so use coarse cut-offs and re-test after changes. Corrective RAG adds a retrieval evaluator that triggers a fallback when retrieved evidence looks poor, a pattern you can reuse for abstention [[r25]]. Good abstention is useful: say what is missing and offer a next step."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w06-t03-d1","title":"Answerable, cited, supported or abstain","alt":"A question is retrieved, an answerability gate decides, the model generates with citations, and a support check validates the cited spans. Failures at either gate lead to abstention or repair.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Question] --> R[Retrieve]\n  R --> A{Answerable}\n  A -->|yes| G[Generate with citations]\n  G --> V{Spans support claims}\n  V -->|yes| O[Answer]\n  V -->|no| X[Abstain or repair]\n  A -->|no| X"}],"whereToUse":["Regulated assistants where every statement must be traceable to a document","Customer support bots that must not invent policies or prices","Search-plus-summary products that show source links","Enterprise Q&A where \"no answer found\" is a valid, logged outcome","Evaluation suites that score citation precision and recall"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use an answerability gate when retrieval often returns weak evidence","Use API-native or schema-based citations so every claim carries a span reference","Use support checks (NLI, quote match) because citations alone are not proof","Use coarse retrieval-score cut-offs plus a measured abstention rate"],"avoid":["Avoid treating the presence of a citation as evidence of correctness","Avoid hard-coded fine thresholds on reranker scores that drift","Avoid abstaining with a bare refusal: offer a next step"],"flips":["You need structured JSON output: Anthropic citations return HTTP 400 with it, so put quote and source fields in your schema and verify them","Retrieval is high quality and the domain is low risk: a prompt permitting \"I don't know\" may replace a separate gate","False abstentions hurt more than errors: answer with a caveat and a confidence label instead of refusing"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Technique","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Answerability classifier before generation","Weak retrieval is common","Latency budget is tiny","Extra call, misses partial answers"],["API-native citations","Plain-text answers over provided documents","You also need structured outputs","Vendor-specific, HTTP 400 with structured outputs"],["Schema fields for quote and source id","JSON outputs, programmatic checks","Free-form prose answers","You validate quotes yourself"],["Reranker score threshold","A cheap first signal for no-answer","Scores drift after model updates","Coarse only, needs re-testing"],["Prompt-only citations","Prototype, low risk","Audit or regulated use","No guarantee spans exist or support the claim"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Citations look right but the cited passage does not support the claim","cause":"Citation proves provenance, not entailment","fix":"Verify the quote exists and run an entailment check against the cited span"},{"symptom":"Requests fail with HTTP 400 after turning on citations","cause":"Anthropic citations cannot be combined with structured outputs","fix":"Use schema fields for quotes and sources or run formatting as a separate step"},{"symptom":"Abstention rate jumps after a ranker update","cause":"A fine-grained score cut-off was tuned on the old score distribution","fix":"Use coarse thresholds, re-test after model changes and alert on abstention drift"},{"symptom":"Users call the bot unhelpful","cause":"Over-abstention on a query type the threshold was never tuned for","fix":"Measure false abstentions per query type and answer with caveats where risk allows"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t03-s1","title":"Benefits guidance in a regulated setting","situation":"An assistant explains benefits rules to staff. A wrong answer can lead to a compliance incident. Retrieval sometimes returns weakly related chunks. An extra second of latency is fine.","constraints":["High cost of wrong answers","Weak retrieval at times","Audit wants source spans","1 extra second allowed"],"unknowns":["How often is the evidence insufficient?","Is there a human escalation route?"],"question":"How do you enforce evidence?","options":[{"name":"Answerability gate, citations required on every claim, span support check, abstain with escalation","verdict":"best","why":"It stops unsupported answers at three points and leaves an audit trail.","tradeoff":"More latency and some false abstentions."},{"name":"Prompt asking the model to cite and say \"I don't know\"","verdict":"poor","why":"Nothing verifies that spans exist or support claims, so wrong answers pass.","tradeoff":"Right for a low-risk prototype."},{"name":"Citations plus a reranker-score cut-off","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and removes the weakest cases.","tradeoff":"Does not test support, and the cut-off drifts with ranker updates."}],"flips":[{"if":"Stakes drop to a general FAQ","then":"A prompt with citations and a coarse cut-off is enough."},{"if":"False abstentions dominate complaints","then":"Answer with caveats where a claim is partially supported."}],"takeaway":"In high-stakes domains, verify support and design the abstention path."},{"id":"w06-t03-s2","title":"Extraction pipeline with JSON output","situation":"A pipeline extracts obligations from policy documents into JSON for a database. Each field must reference the source text. The team planned to use API-native citations.","constraints":["Structured JSON output required","Each field needs source evidence","Batch processing","Quote check can be automated"],"unknowns":["Is the model API fixed?","Are quotes short enough to string-match?"],"question":"How do you attach evidence?","options":[{"name":"Add `quote` and `source_id` fields to the JSON schema and verify quotes by string match","verdict":"best","why":"It works with structured output and lets code verify evidence for every field.","tradeoff":"Quotes lengthen output and you maintain the verifier."},{"name":"API-native citations in one call, then a second call to format as JSON","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps native span locations and valid JSON.","tradeoff":"Two calls and extra latency and cost."},{"name":"API-native citations together with structured outputs in one request","verdict":"poor","why":"That combination returns HTTP 400.","tradeoff":"Right when the output is plain text, not structured."}],"flips":[{"if":"Output can be plain text","then":"API-native citations are simplest."},{"if":"Quotes are long or paraphrased","then":"Use an entailment check instead of string match."}],"takeaway":"Pick the citation mechanism that your output format allows."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"After enabling Anthropic citations on a request that also uses structured outputs, what happens?","options":["Citations are silently dropped","Output tokens double","The model ignores the schema","The request returns HTTP 400"],"answer":3,"explain":"The documentation states the two features are incompatible and enabling both returns HTTP 400. Silent dropping and token doubling are not documented.","ref":"r24"},{"id":"w06-t03-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which statement about citations is correct?","options":["A citation proves the claim is true","A citation removes the need for abstention","A citation proves provenance, not that the passage entails the claim","A citation guarantees the retrieved set was complete"],"answer":2,"explain":"A citation shows where text came from. Whether the span supports the claim needs a separate check, and completeness and abstention are unrelated to citing.","ref":"r23"},{"id":"w06-t03-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the steps of an evidence-enforced answer.","items":["Retrieve evidence","Decide whether it is answerable","Generate with citations","Check that cited spans support the claims","Answer or abstain with a next step"],"explain":"Answerability comes before generation, and support checks come after, so unsupported text never reaches the user."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Provenance versus entailment?","back":"A citation shows where text came from (provenance). Entailment asks whether the cited span supports the claim and needs its own check."},{"front":"Which Azure reranker facts matter for abstention thresholds?","back":"Scores run 0 to 4, only the top 50 candidates are reranked and distributions shift with model updates. Use coarse cut-offs and re-test."},{"front":"What do Anthropic citations not combine with?","back":"Structured outputs. Enabling both returns HTTP 400. They do work with prompt caching, token counting and batch."}],"related":["w06-t02","w06-t06","w06-t10","w05-t06","w05-t16","w06-l2"],"refs":["r22","r23","r24","r25","tr1"]},{"id":"w06-t04","order":4,"group":"Azure AI Content Safety","title":"Harm categories and severities","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Azure AI Content Safety classifies text and images into four harm categories with severity levels. A guardrail control compares the severity to a threshold you choose, so the threshold is your false-positive dial.","useIf":"You need a managed first line of defence for hate, sexual, violence and self-harm content in prompts and completions.","avoidIf":"Your risk is a custom policy (for example investment advice) or prompt injection: these categories do not cover them.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The four categories are **hate and fairness, sexual, violence and self-harm**, for text and images [[r6]]. In Foundry guardrails each category has four severity levels. **Safe** is annotated but never filtered and is not configurable. **Low** is mild material (prejudiced views, mild depictions in fictional contexts, personal experiences). **Medium** is moderate (graphic depictions, bullying, content promoting harmful acts). **High** is severe (extremist content, explicit depictions, content endorsing serious harm) [[r6]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Threshold behaviour in a Foundry guardrail control","head":["Threshold","What it flags","Effect on users"],"rows":[["Low","Low severity and above","Flags the most content, more false positives"],["Medium","Medium severity and above","Default balance"],["High","Only the most severe content","Flags the least, more misses"],["Off","Nothing","Only for customers approved for modified guardrails"]]},{"t":"p","md":"New model deployments get the default guardrail `Microsoft.DefaultV2`: hate, violence, sexual and self-harm at Medium on prompts and completions, plus other controls. It applies to all Foundry Models sold by Azure except audio transcription models. A request over a threshold is blocked with an error that names the category [[r2]]. The standalone service returns a 0 to 7 scale for text, or on request the trimmed scale 0, 2, 4, 6, where each pair maps to one level ([0,1] to 0, [2,3] to 2, [4,5] to 4, [6,7] to 6). The image model returns only 0, 2, 4 and 6 [[r6]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Read the behaviour column, not the adjectives","md":"The documentation calls Low the least restrictive setting and High the most restrictive, yet Low flags the most content. Decide by what each threshold flags, as in the table above [[r6]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Text analyze input","value":"10K chars","note":"Content Safety limit [[r10]]"},{"label":"Image","value":"4 MB","note":"50x50 to 7200x7200 pixels"},{"label":"Multimodal text","value":"1K chars","note":"Per request"},{"label":"Tested languages","value":"8","note":"Other languages may differ in accuracy and false positives"}]}],"whereToUse":["Consumer chat assistants that must refuse hateful, sexual, violent or self-harm content","Education and youth products where thresholds should be strict","Professional tools where clinical or legal text uses graphic vocabulary and needs looser settings","Image-capable assistants that moderate uploads and generated images","Audit evidence: annotations record category and severity per request"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use the default Medium threshold as the starting point and measure","Use Low thresholds where the audience is vulnerable and over-blocking is cheaper than harm","Use High thresholds with extra controls where professional text would be over-blocked","Use annotations to log severities even when you do not block"],"avoid":["Avoid expecting these categories to catch prompt injection, protected text or custom policy","Avoid assuming equal accuracy in every language: eight were trained and tested","Avoid switching a category Off without approval and a replacement control"],"flips":["Audience becomes children: lower thresholds to Low and add human review","Users become verified professionals: raise thresholds for the categories their work involves","Policy is custom (domain rules): add custom controls or an LLM or classifier you evaluate"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Foundry guardrail harm controls","Standard harm categories, managed service","Custom taxonomy or unsupported languages","Threshold tuning, English-centred tests"],["Standalone Content Safety API","You need raw 0 to 7 scores in your own logic","You want managed block behaviour","You build the action layer"],["Custom categories (Content Safety)","Emerging or domain-specific harms","You rely on the standard model","Standard is retired or retiring per its page, rapid is preview"],["Llama Guard or an LLM classifier","Self-hosting and rewritable categories","You lack GPUs or evaluation data","Operations and calibration"],["Deterministic lists and rules","Exact banned terms or formats","Paraphrase and context matter","Easy to evade, many false negatives"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A medical assistant blocks legitimate clinical questions","cause":"The default Medium threshold flags graphic but professional vocabulary","fix":"Raise the threshold for the affected categories for verified users and add other controls"},{"symptom":"Non-English users report more wrongful blocks","cause":"Detection accuracy and false positive rates can vary outside the eight tested languages","fix":"Build a per-language evaluation set and tune per language"},{"symptom":"A long prompt is not fully moderated","cause":"Documentation mentions moderation of the first 1,000 characters in some scenarios and limits differ by API (wording unclear, UNVERIFIED)","fix":"Test with long prompts and chunk inputs yourself"},{"symptom":"Team confused about which threshold is stricter","cause":"The docs call Low least restrictive although it flags the most","fix":"Document thresholds by what they flag"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t04-s1","title":"Homework helper for children","situation":"A learning assistant is used by children aged 8 to 12 through a school. Parents expect strict filtering. Refusing a harmless question is a minor annoyance.","constraints":["Young audience","Over-blocking is cheap","Text and image uploads","Teachers can review flagged items"],"unknowns":["Which languages are used?","Who reviews appeals?"],"question":"How do you set the harm controls?","options":[{"name":"Low thresholds on all four categories for input and output, with a human review route","verdict":"best","why":"Low flags the most content, which suits an audience where harm is costly and false positives are cheap.","tradeoff":"More wrongful blocks and review load."},{"name":"Default Medium thresholds plus a topic allow list","verdict":"good","why":"Balanced, with the allow list narrowing scope.","tradeoff":"Mild but unsuitable content may pass."},{"name":"High thresholds to reduce annoyance","verdict":"poor","why":"High flags only the most severe content, letting mild material through to children.","tradeoff":"Right for a professional tool with legitimate graphic content."}],"flips":[{"if":"The product becomes adult general-purpose","then":"Return to Medium and measure false positives."},{"if":"Non-English traffic grows","then":"Add per-language tests before trusting thresholds."}],"takeaway":"Set thresholds by who is harmed and what over-blocking costs."},{"id":"w06-t04-s2","title":"Clinical documentation assistant","situation":"Clinicians use an assistant to draft notes that discuss self-harm, violence and injury in clinical terms. The default guardrail blocks many legitimate drafts. Users are authenticated staff.","constraints":["Authenticated professionals","Clinical vocabulary is legitimate","Blocking disrupts care documentation","Audit trail required"],"unknowns":["Is modified-guardrails approval obtainable?","Which categories cause most blocks?"],"question":"How do you tune the controls?","options":[{"name":"Raise thresholds on the affected categories, annotate and log, and add output review for risky patterns","verdict":"best","why":"It removes wrongful blocks while keeping the most severe content flagged and logged.","tradeoff":"More misses on moderate content, covered by review."},{"name":"Apply for modified guardrails and turn categories Off","verdict":"good","why":"Removes blocking entirely where it is approved.","tradeoff":"Approval is required and you lose the safety net."},{"name":"Keep the default Medium thresholds","verdict":"poor","why":"It keeps blocking legitimate clinical text.","tradeoff":"Right for a public consumer product."}],"flips":[{"if":"The tool opens to the public","then":"Return to Medium or Low thresholds."},{"if":"Blocks come from one category only","then":"Tune that category only."}],"takeaway":"Tune per category and audience, and keep logging even when you loosen."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"The text model is asked for the trimmed scale (0, 2, 4, 6). A text has a full-scale severity of 5. What trimmed value is returned?","answer":4,"tolerancePct":0,"explain":"Each pair maps to one level: [4,5] maps to 4. So severity 5 becomes 4.","ref":"r6"},{"id":"w06-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which threshold in a Foundry control flags the most content?","options":["High","Medium","Low","Off"],"answer":2,"explain":"Low flags severity low and above. High flags only the most severe, Medium sits between and Off disables detection. The docs' adjectives can mislead, so read what each flags.","ref":"r6"},{"id":"w06-t04-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements about the default guardrail are correct?","options":["It is named `Microsoft.DefaultV2`","It sets hate, violence, sexual and self-harm to Medium","It applies to audio transcription models","A request over threshold is blocked with an error naming the category"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The default guardrail uses Medium for the four categories and blocks with a named category. It does not apply to audio transcription models.","ref":"r2"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Four harm categories in Azure AI Content Safety?","back":"Hate and fairness, sexual, violence and self-harm, for text and images."},{"front":"Standalone severity scales?","back":"Text 0 to 7 (or trimmed 0, 2, 4, 6 with pairs merged). Image model returns only 0, 2, 4, 6."},{"front":"What does the default guardrail do?","back":"`Microsoft.DefaultV2` sets the four harm categories to Medium on prompts and completions, and blocks with an error naming the category."}],"related":["w06-t05","w06-t07","w06-t10","w06-l3","w14-t13"],"refs":["r2","r6","r7","r10"]},{"id":"w06-t05","order":5,"group":"Azure AI Content Safety","title":"Prompt Shields","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Prompt Shields detects two attack families: user prompt attacks typed by the caller and document attacks hidden in content the model reads. It is a probabilistic classifier, one layer of a defence in depth.","useIf":"Your model reads text you do not control: user input, retrieved documents, web pages, emails or tool output.","avoidIf":"You expect it to be a complete defence: pair it with least privilege, Spotlighting where available and deterministic blocks on exfiltration paths.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**User prompt attacks** (formerly jailbreak risk detection) try to override the system rules. The documented subtypes are changing system rules, conversation mockup, role-play and encoding. **Document attacks** (indirect attacks) hide instructions in content such as a retrieved page or an email: the subtypes include manipulated content, system access, information gathering, availability, fraud and malware, plus the four user-attack types. Prompt Shields has been GA since August 2024 [[r3]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Two shields, where they look","head":["Shield","Scans","Foundry scan points","Standalone API"],"rows":[["User prompt attacks","What the caller typed","User input","One `userPrompt`"],["Document attacks","Retrieved or tool-returned text","User input and tool response","Up to five `documents` per call"]]},{"t":"p","md":"In Foundry guardrails the annotations carry `detected` and `filtered` booleans. In the standalone API you send one `userPrompt` and up to five `documents` and read `attackDetected`. The prompt limit is 10K characters, and Prompt Shields was tested on English only, so test other languages yourself [[r3]] [[r10]]. A top-12 retrieval therefore needs three calls, which you can run in parallel to protect latency."},{"t":"p","md":"**Spotlighting** is a separate, Azure preview feature for document attacks. It marks documents as lower trust by base64 encoding them. It is off by default, works with the Chat Completions API on models only (not agents), raises token counts and can push a long document over the input limit, and the model may mention base64 in answers [[r3]]. The Microsoft paper reports attack success falling from above 50 percent to below 2 percent on GPT-family models with minimal task impact [[r12]]. The MSRC defence in depth guidance combines prompt hardening, Spotlighting, Prompt Shields and deterministic blocking of exfiltration paths [[r11]]."}],"whereToUse":["RAG assistants that retrieve from wikis, tickets, shared drives or the web","Mail and calendar copilots where content comes from outside senders","Agents whose tools return web or file content","Public chat endpoints that face deliberate jailbreak attempts","Security reviews that map untrusted inputs to a scan point"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use the user-attack shield on every public prompt","Use the document-attack shield on retrieved chunks and tool responses when sources are not fully trusted","Use Spotlighting on models where preview is acceptable and token cost is fine","Combine with least-privilege tools and deterministic exfiltration blocks"],"avoid":["Avoid relying on the shield alone: it is probabilistic and tested on English","Avoid Spotlighting for very long documents near the input limit","Avoid assuming Spotlighting protects agents: it is not supported for them"],"flips":["Sources are a curated internal library: sample or relax document scanning, keep user-attack scanning","The workload is an agent rather than a model call: use the tool-response control instead of Spotlighting","Traffic is not English: build a local attack test set and add heuristics or classifiers"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Defence","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prompt Shields","Managed detection of user and document attacks","Non-English or novel attacks","Probabilistic, English-tested, extra call"],["Spotlighting","Documents are lower trust and fit the limit","Agents, long documents","Preview, base64 tokens, models only"],["Prompt hardening alone","Zero-cost baseline","Determined attackers","Weak by itself"],["Least privilege and egress blocks","Tools can act or leak","Pure Q&A with no tools","Design effort, restricts features"],["Open-source or LLM classifier","Self-hosted or custom attack types","No evaluation data","You calibrate and operate it"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A planted instruction in a retrieved page changes the answer","cause":"Only the user prompt was scanned","fix":"Pass retrieved chunks as `documents` or enable the indirect attack control on tool responses"},{"symptom":"Long documents fail after enabling Spotlighting","cause":"Base64 encoding raises token count past the input limit","fix":"Shorten or chunk the documents, or skip Spotlighting for those requests"},{"symptom":"Answers mention base64","cause":"The model sees encoded text and sometimes refers to it","fix":"Instruct the model about the encoding and test output wording"},{"symptom":"Non-English attacks pass","cause":"Prompt Shields was tested on English only","fix":"Create a multilingual attack test set and add complementary controls"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t05-s1","title":"Q&A over scraped web pages, no tools","situation":"An assistant answers from web pages the team crawls. It has no tools and returns 12 chunks per query. Latency budget is two seconds. Some pages may carry hidden instructions.","constraints":["Untrusted pages","No tools","12 chunks per query","2 second budget"],"unknowns":["Is the model in the Chat Completions API?","How long are the chunks?"],"question":"What do you add for document attacks?","options":[{"name":"Scan chunks with the document shield in three parallel calls of up to five, plus an output guard","verdict":"best","why":"It matches the five-documents-per-call limit and parallelism keeps the added latency near one call.","tradeoff":"Three calls per query and some false positives on benign pages."},{"name":"Spotlighting only","verdict":"good","why":"Lowers attack success in the research and costs nothing per call.","tradeoff":"Preview, adds base64 tokens and does not detect or report attacks."},{"name":"Tell the model in the system prompt to ignore document instructions","verdict":"poor","why":"It is the weakest control and attackers target exactly this.","tradeoff":"Fine as one layer, never as the only layer."}],"flips":[{"if":"Pages come from a curated internal library","then":"Sample scanning or scan only new documents."},{"if":"Chunks are very long","then":"Skip Spotlighting for them and rely on shield scanning."}],"takeaway":"Scan what the model reads, in parallel batches that match the API limits."},{"id":"w06-t05-s2","title":"Foundry agent that calls a web-fetch tool","situation":"An agent in Foundry Agent Service fetches web pages through a tool and can write notes to a shared store. Preview features are acceptable for this pilot.","constraints":["Foundry agent, not a bare model","Tool returns untrusted text","Write access to a shared store","Preview acceptable"],"unknowns":["Which domains may the tool fetch?","Who reads the shared store?"],"question":"Which controls do you rely on?","options":[{"name":"Indirect-attack control on tool responses, tool-call controls and a domain and write allow list","verdict":"best","why":"The document shield scans tool responses in Foundry, and tool-call checks plus allow lists limit damage if one slips.","tradeoff":"Tool-level controls are preview, and allow lists restrict flexibility."},{"name":"Prompt hardening plus output filtering","verdict":"good","why":"Easy and adds a layer.","tradeoff":"The side effect (writing to the store) happens before output is checked."},{"name":"Spotlighting on the underlying model","verdict":"poor","why":"Spotlighting is not supported for agents.","tradeoff":"Right for plain model calls on the Chat Completions API."}],"flips":[{"if":"Preview features are not allowed","then":"Implement tool response scanning and allow lists in your own code with the standalone API."},{"if":"The tool becomes read-only and the store is private","then":"Reduce to tool-response scanning."}],"takeaway":"For agents, scan tool responses and limit what a hijacked agent can do."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t05-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"The standalone Prompt Shields API accepts up to five documents per call. How many calls do you need to scan 12 retrieved chunks?","answer":3,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"calls","explain":"12 divided by 5 is 2.4, so 3 calls (5, 5 and 2). They can run in parallel, so added latency is about one call.","ref":"r3"},{"id":"w06-t05-q2","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"In Foundry, where are document (indirect) attacks scanned?","options":["At user input and tool response","At model output only","At the embedding step","Only at tool call"],"answer":0,"explain":"User attacks are scanned at user input, and document attacks at user input and tool response. Output and embedding steps are not documented scan points for the shield, and tool call is a separate control.","ref":"r3"},{"id":"w06-t05-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are true about Spotlighting in Azure?","options":["It is off by default","It base64 encodes documents","It works with agents","It can raise token counts and push long documents over the limit"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Spotlighting is off by default, base64 encodes documents and raises token counts. It is supported for models on the Chat Completions API, not agents.","ref":"r3"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Two Prompt Shields attack families?","back":"User prompt attacks (formerly jailbreak detection) and document attacks (indirect), the latter hidden in retrieved or tool-returned content."},{"front":"Standalone Prompt Shields request shape?","back":"One `userPrompt` and up to five `documents`. The response carries `attackDetected`. Prompt limit 10K characters."},{"front":"Spotlighting trade-offs?","back":"Preview, base64 encodes documents, models only (not agents), raises tokens. Paper: attack success from above 50% to below 2%."}],"related":["w06-t01","w06-t07","w06-t10","w07-t02","w07-t17","w06-l3"],"refs":["r3","r10","r11","r12"]},{"id":"w06-t06","order":6,"group":"Azure AI Content Safety","title":"Groundedness detection and protected material","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Two Content Safety checks guard the output: groundedness detection compares an answer with its sources, and protected material detection flags known copyrighted text and code. Their status and limits decide where you can use them.","useIf":"You need managed output checks for unsupported claims or reproduced copyrighted content and can live with their language, size and release-state limits.","avoidIf":"Your traffic is not English or your content exceeds the size limits: both features were tested on English only.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Groundedness detection** checks an answer against supplied grounding sources. You choose a domain (`Medical` or `Generic`) and a task (`Summarization` or `QnA`). Non-reasoning mode returns a fast grounded or ungrounded verdict, reasoning mode explains ungrounded segments, and optional correction returns repaired text. It is **preview** and English only, grounding sources are limited to 55,000 characters, text plus query to 7,500 characters, and the query must be at least 3 words. In Foundry guardrails it applies to models, not agents [[r4]] [[r10]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Protected material detection** scans completions. The text filter flags known text such as song lyrics, articles, recipes and selected web content. The code filter flags matches to GitHub repositories, and its code index is current only through 2023-04-06. Both are English only, and a completion needs at least 110 characters to be scanned. The docs quote flag thresholds of 40 characters for recipes, 200 characters for news and 11 words for lyrics [[r5]]. Text reached GA in August 2024. The code filter was labelled preview in a September 2024 notice, and its current status is UNVERIFIED: the Foundry risk table (checked 2026-10-13) lists it for models and agents without a preview label [[r1]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"What exists and its status on 2026-10-13","head":["Feature","Detects","Status","Language","Key limit"],"rows":[["Groundedness detection","Claims unsupported by sources","Preview","English","55,000 and 7,500 characters"],["Protected material, text","Known copyrighted text","GA (August 2024)","English","110 characters minimum"],["Protected material, code","Matches to GitHub code","UNVERIFIED","English","Index through 2023-04-06"],["Task adherence","Misaligned or premature tool use","Preview (since 2025-11)","See page","100K characters"],["Custom categories, standard","Your own taxonomy (trained)","Retiring or retired","English","Page says retires 2026-09-01"],["Custom categories, rapid","Emerging incidents","Preview","Text and image","LLM based"]]}],"whereToUse":["Summarisation and Q&A features where an unsupported sentence is costly and content is English","Content generators (marketing, documentation) that must not reproduce lyrics, articles or recipes","Code assistants where licence exposure of generated snippets matters","Evaluation: reasoning mode during development to see which segments fail","Annotate-only monitoring of how often outputs are flagged"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use groundedness detection on English output with sources that fit the limits and preview is acceptable","Use the protected material text filter on generated prose that might repeat known text","Use annotations and sampling to estimate flag rates before blocking","Use reasoning mode in development and non-reasoning mode in production"],"avoid":["Avoid these features for non-English content without your own evaluation","Avoid treating the code filter as legal clearance: its index ends on 2023-04-06","Avoid sending answers or sources beyond the size limits"],"flips":["Preview status is not allowed: use an NLI model or LLM judge for groundedness","Generated code ships to customers: add licence scanning in CI on top of the filter","Answers exceed 7,500 characters: split them into segments with matching sources"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Groundedness detection API","English, short sources, managed service","Other languages, big sources, GA required","Preview, size limits, models only in Foundry"],["NLI model or LLM judge","Any language, custom rubric","Latency and hosting cost matter","Calibration effort, judge bias"],["Protected material filter","Prose output may repeat known text","Content is not English","Does not prove originality, code index dated"],["Similarity search against your own licensed corpus","You own the reference set","You need broad web coverage","Index build and upkeep"],["Human or legal review","Published or high-value content","High volume","Slow, costly, but accountable"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Groundedness calls fail on long answers","cause":"Text plus query exceeds 7,500 characters or sources exceed 55,000 characters","fix":"Segment the answer and select only the relevant sources"},{"symptom":"Short snippets pass protected material checks","cause":"A completion needs at least 110 characters to be scanned and thresholds differ by content type","fix":"Do not rely on it for short fragments, add other controls"},{"symptom":"Generated code resembles recent open-source code but is not flagged","cause":"The code index is current only through 2023-04-06","fix":"Run licence and similarity scanning in CI"},{"symptom":"A feature disappears or behaves differently","cause":"Preview or retiring features change, and the custom categories page still says it is retiring after 2026-09-01","fix":"Confirm status in the portal and keep a fallback"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t06-s1","title":"Internal code assistant with licensing exposure","situation":"Developers use an assistant to generate code that ships in products. Legal worries about copied open-source snippets. The team wants managed checks and a clear audit trail.","constraints":["Generated code ships to customers","Licence exposure matters","Managed service preferred","English prompts"],"unknowns":["Is the code filter's status acceptable for production?","Is a CI licence scanner already in use?"],"question":"What do you rely on?","options":[{"name":"Protected material code filter in annotate mode plus licence and similarity scanning in CI","verdict":"best","why":"The filter adds a signal and the CI scan covers code newer than the filter's 2023-04-06 index.","tradeoff":"Extra pipeline step and review of flagged snippets."},{"name":"The code filter alone","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and managed.","tradeoff":"Stale index and unverified status leave gaps."},{"name":"A prompt that says do not copy code","verdict":"poor","why":"It gives no detection and no audit evidence.","tradeoff":"A weak extra layer, never a control by itself."}],"flips":[{"if":"Code is only for internal throwaway scripts","then":"The filter in annotate mode is enough."},{"if":"Legal needs formal sign-off","then":"Add a documented review step for flagged output."}],"takeaway":"A dated index is a reason to add an independent check."},{"id":"w06-t06-s2","title":"Long English summaries of medical notes","situation":"A tool produces summaries up to 20,000 characters from source notes of about 40,000 characters. Unsupported statements are unacceptable. Preview features are allowed in this pilot.","constraints":["Answers beyond 7,500 characters","Sources within 55,000 characters","English","Preview accepted"],"unknowns":["Can the summary be split by section?","What latency is acceptable?"],"question":"How do you use groundedness detection?","options":[{"name":"Split the summary into segments, pair each with its source notes, run non-reasoning mode and log failures","verdict":"best","why":"It respects the 7,500 and 55,000 character limits and gives a verdict per segment.","tradeoff":"More calls and the need to align segments to sources."},{"name":"Send the whole summary in one call","verdict":"poor","why":"It exceeds the 7,500 character limit for text plus query.","tradeoff":"Fine for short answers under the limit."},{"name":"Use reasoning mode in production for every segment","verdict":"good","why":"Explains which parts are ungrounded.","tradeoff":"Slower and costlier. The docs suggest it for development."}],"flips":[{"if":"Preview is not allowed","then":"Use claim-level NLI or an LLM judge."},{"if":"Notes are not English","then":"Use a judge evaluated in that language."}],"takeaway":"Fit the check to the service limits by segmenting."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t06-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Text plus query is limited to 7,500 characters. Your query is 100 characters. A 20,000 character answer is split into equal segments. What is the minimum number of segments?","answer":3,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"segments","explain":"Each segment can hold at most 7,400 characters of text. 20,000 divided by 7,400 is 2.7, so 3 segments.","ref":"r4"},{"id":"w06-t06-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Up to which date is the protected material code index current, per the documentation?","options":["2021-09-30","2022-12-31","2023-04-06","2024-08-01"],"answer":2,"explain":"The docs state the code index is current only through 2023-04-06. The other dates are not documented for it.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w06-t06-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Which statements about these checks are correct?","options":["Groundedness detection is preview and English only","Protected material needs at least 110 characters in a completion","Groundedness detection applies to agents in Foundry guardrails","Protected material text reached GA in August 2024"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Groundedness is preview, English only and applies to models, not agents. Protected material text is GA and needs 110 characters.","ref":"r4"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Groundedness detection limits?","back":"Sources 55,000 characters, text plus query 7,500, query at least 3 words, English only, preview, models not agents."},{"front":"Protected material details?","back":"Text and code filters on completions, English only, minimum 110 characters. Code index current only through 2023-04-06."},{"front":"Reasoning versus non-reasoning groundedness mode?","back":"Non-reasoning returns a fast verdict. Reasoning explains ungrounded segments, and the docs suggest it for development."}],"related":["w06-t02","w06-t03","w06-t07","w06-l3","w05-t16"],"refs":["r1","r4","r5","r7","r10"]},{"id":"w06-t07","order":7,"group":"Azure AI Content Safety","title":"Platform guardrails in Azure OpenAI and Foundry","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A Foundry guardrail is a named set of controls. Each control names a risk, the intervention points to scan and an action. Models and agents follow different rules, and streaming mode decides when violating text can appear.","useIf":"You deploy models or agents in Microsoft Foundry and want managed filtering configured as policy, not code.","avoidIf":"Your agents run outside Foundry Agent Service or your policy needs custom taxonomies: guardrails do not cover other registered agents.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **guardrail** is a named collection of controls. Each control defines a risk, the intervention points to scan (user input, tool call, tool response, output) and the response action: **annotate** or **annotate and block**. Agents support only annotate and block. Tool call and tool response controls are preview and for agents only. The system applies to all Foundry Models sold by Azure except audio transcription models, and to agents built in Foundry Agent Service, not to other agents in the Foundry Control Plane [[r1]]. New models get `Microsoft.DefaultV2` [[r2]]. Creating guardrails needs the Foundry Account Owner role, renamed from Azure AI Account Owner [[r1]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Which risks apply to models and agents (Foundry guardrails page, checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Risk","Models","Agents (preview)"],"rows":[["Hate, sexual, self-harm, violence","Yes","Yes"],["User prompt attacks, indirect attacks","Yes","Yes"],["Protected material (text, code)","Yes","Yes"],["PII (preview)","Yes","Yes"],["Task adherence (preview)","Yes","Yes"],["Spotlighting (preview)","Yes","No"],["Groundedness (preview)","Yes","No"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"An agent guardrail replaces the model guardrail","md":"Risks are detected in an agent using the guardrail assigned to the agent, not the one on its underlying model. With no custom guardrail the agent inherits the model's. Controls for preview risks that agents do not yet support are ignored for the agent but still apply to models that share the guardrail [[r1]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Streaming.** The default mode buffers completion text, filters each buffer and returns content chunks, not single tokens. The **asynchronous filter** streams token by token with no added filter latency, sends annotations in separate messages with offsets, and guarantees the stop signal only within about a 1,000 character window, so some violating text can reach the user first. Changing the mode needs permission to modify content filtering policies [[r9]]. The Foundry **PII filter** scans model output only, is preview, and its block action blocks the whole output [[r8]]."}],"whereToUse":["Applying one policy consistently across many model deployments and agents","Meeting an organisation's baseline for harm, jailbreak and protected-material controls","Giving security teams a named policy object to review and audit","Choosing between buffered and asynchronous streaming for a chat UI","Adding tool-level scanning to Foundry agents that call external tools"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use the default guardrail as a baseline and add controls by risk","Use agent guardrails with tool call and tool response controls for agents with tools","Use buffered streaming when showing violating text even briefly is unacceptable","Use annotate mode first to measure flag rates before blocking"],"avoid":["Avoid assuming a model guardrail covers an agent that has its own guardrail","Avoid enabling groundedness or Spotlighting controls and expecting them to run on agents","Avoid the asynchronous filter for audiences where early exposure is unacceptable"],"flips":["Latency matters more than brief exposure and the audience is internal: asynchronous streaming is acceptable","Agents run outside Foundry Agent Service: implement guards in your own pipeline","Policy needs custom categories or languages not tested by the service: add your own classifier"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Foundry guardrails on a model","Managed policy for model calls","Agent tool behaviour matters","Tool controls do not apply to models"],["Foundry guardrails on an agent (preview)","Agent in Foundry Agent Service with tools","Agent registered elsewhere","Preview, block-only action, replaces model guardrail"],["Standalone Content Safety APIs","Any app or platform, custom action logic","You want zero code","You orchestrate calls and actions"],["Open-source rails (NeMo, Guardrails AI)","Custom flows, self-hosting","You need a managed service","Operations and evaluation burden"],["Deterministic app-side controls","Allow lists, schemas, egress limits","You need semantic detection","No detection of novel content"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Tool calls are unscanned after creating an agent guardrail","cause":"The agent's guardrail fully overrides the model's and contained no tool controls","fix":"Add tool call and tool response controls to the agent guardrail"},{"symptom":"A groundedness control seems inactive on an agent","cause":"Groundedness and Spotlighting are not applicable to agents, so the control does not take effect","fix":"Check the applicability table and add a separate groundedness step for agents"},{"symptom":"Users briefly see disallowed text while streaming","cause":"Asynchronous filter signals the stop only within about 1,000 characters","fix":"Use buffered streaming for sensitive audiences, or hide text client-side until clear"},{"symptom":"An agent has no guardrail despite the organisation policy","cause":"The agent is registered in the Foundry Control Plane but not built in Foundry Agent Service","fix":"Add guards in the agent's own pipeline or rebuild it in the service"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t07-s1","title":"Streaming chat for teenagers","situation":"A public chat product for teenagers streams responses for a responsive feel. Showing even a few harmful sentences is unacceptable to the business. Latency complaints are secondary.","constraints":["Young audience","No tolerance for brief exposure","Streaming UX wanted","Foundry model deployment"],"unknowns":["How long are typical answers?","Can the client delay rendering?"],"question":"Which streaming mode do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Default buffered streaming with filtering on each buffer","verdict":"best","why":"Content is filtered before chunks reach the user, so no violating text is shown.","tradeoff":"Chunked delivery feels less smooth."},{"name":"Asynchronous filter with the client hiding text until annotations arrive","verdict":"good","why":"Fast tokens and some protection.","tradeoff":"You build the hiding logic, and the stop signal is only guaranteed within about 1,000 characters."},{"name":"Asynchronous filter with raw rendering","verdict":"poor","why":"Violating text can be displayed before the stop signal.","tradeoff":"Right for an internal tool where brief exposure is tolerable."}],"flips":[{"if":"Audience is internal staff and speed matters","then":"Asynchronous streaming with logging is acceptable."},{"if":"Answers are short","then":"Non-streamed responses avoid the issue entirely."}],"takeaway":"Streaming mode is a decision about tolerated exposure."},{"id":"w06-t07-s2","title":"Foundry agent with external tools","situation":"An agent in Foundry Agent Service calls a CRM tool and a web-fetch tool. The team set guardrails on the model deployment months ago and now builds the agent. Preview features are acceptable.","constraints":["Agent in Foundry Agent Service","Tools return untrusted content","Existing model guardrail","Preview acceptable"],"unknowns":["Which tools write data?","Do all controls the team uses apply to agents?"],"question":"How do you configure the guardrail?","options":[{"name":"A dedicated agent guardrail with user input, tool call, tool response and output controls using annotate and block","verdict":"best","why":"Agents use their own guardrail, and the tool controls are the only managed scan of tool traffic.","tradeoff":"Preview features and block-only actions for agents."},{"name":"Inherit the model's guardrail and enforce tool allow lists in code","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps input and output coverage and limits tool abuse deterministically.","tradeoff":"No managed scan of tool calls and responses."},{"name":"An agent guardrail containing only an output control","verdict":"poor","why":"It replaces the model's controls and leaves input, tool calls and tool responses unscanned.","tradeoff":"Right only for an agent with no tools and trusted input."}],"flips":[{"if":"Preview is not allowed for tool controls","then":"Use deterministic allow lists and the standalone APIs inside your tool wrapper."},{"if":"The agent has no tools","then":"Input and output controls suffice."}],"takeaway":"Review the agent's effective guardrail, not the model's."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A model deployment has a strict violence control. An agent on that model has its own guardrail without a violence control. What applies to the agent?","options":["The model's violence control","Both guardrails combined","Only the agent's guardrail, so no violence control","The default guardrail"],"answer":2,"explain":"The agent's guardrail fully overrides the model's, so the model's violence control does not apply. Guardrails are not combined, and the default applies only when no custom guardrail is assigned.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w06-t07-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"How long is the stop signal guaranteed to arrive in the asynchronous filter?","options":["Before any token is sent","Within one second","Only after the full response","Within about 1,000 characters"],"answer":3,"explain":"The docs guarantee the stop signal only within about a 1,000 character window, so some violating text can be shown first. It is not before tokens, not time-based and not only at the end.","ref":"r9"},{"id":"w06-t07-q3","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which controls are listed as not applicable to agents?","options":["Spotlighting","Groundedness","Task adherence","Hate and violence"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"Spotlighting and groundedness apply to models only. Task adherence and the harm categories apply to both models and agents.","ref":"r1"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a Foundry guardrail?","back":"A named collection of controls. Each defines a risk, intervention points to scan and an action (annotate, or annotate and block)."},{"front":"Agent versus model guardrail?","back":"An agent's guardrail fully overrides its model's. With none assigned, the agent inherits the model's. Applies only to agents built in Foundry Agent Service."},{"front":"Default versus asynchronous streaming filter?","back":"Default buffers and filters each chunk. Asynchronous sends tokens immediately and signals stop only within about 1,000 characters."}],"related":["w06-t01","w06-t04","w06-t05","w06-t06","w06-t09","w12-t01"],"refs":["r1","r2","r8","r9"]},{"id":"w06-t08","order":8,"group":"Alternatives and operations","title":"Open-source guard frameworks","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"NeMo Guardrails orchestrates rails around an LLM, Guardrails AI runs validators on inputs and outputs, and Llama Guard is a safety classifier model. You self-host them when data location, taxonomy or language rule out the managed service.","useIf":"Data cannot leave your environment, your policy taxonomy is custom, or you need flow control and validation logic the platform guardrails do not offer.","avoidIf":"A managed guardrail already meets the need: self-hosting adds GPUs, evaluation work and upgrade churn.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**NeMo Guardrails** is an open-source Python library, also deployable as a Kubernetes microservice with the same YAML and Colang configuration. It names five rail types: input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output. Built-ins cover content safety, jailbreak detection, topic control, PII handling and fact-checking, with integrations for Llama Guard, NemoGuard models and third-party APIs. Its `self check facts` output rail uses `$relevant_chunks` as evidence and returns a 0.0 to 1.0 score that depends on the LLM following the prompt. If a reasoning model spends its `max_tokens` budget before answering, the rail fails closed (score 0.0, response blocked) [[r13]] [[r14]]. The docs list releases v0.22.0 to v0.24.1 plus a Latest build, so pin a version."},{"t":"p","md":"**Guardrails AI** is a Python framework that runs Input and Output Guards built from validators in Guardrails Hub, and also produces structured output. The Hub showed 65 validators on 2026-10-13 (for example ban list, toxic language, detect jailbreak and detect PII, which uses Microsoft Presidio), and that count changes often [[r15]] [[r29]] [[r17]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Llama Guard** is a safety classifier model. Llama Guard 4 has 12B parameters, is natively multimodal (early fusion), runs on one GPU and is a drop-in for Llama Guard 3 8B and 11B. Llama Guard 3 1B remains useful at the edge. It classifies prompt text and an image together, not an image alone. Llama Guard 3 uses categories S1 Violent Crimes to S14 Code Interpreter Abuse, and the category text in the prompt can be rewritten, which is how you encode a custom taxonomy [[r16]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"NeMo rail types","value":"5","note":"Input, retrieval, dialog, execution, output"},{"label":"Guardrails Hub validators","value":"65","note":"Count on 2026-10-13, changes often"},{"label":"Llama Guard 4 size","value":"12B","note":"Natively multimodal, one GPU"},{"label":"Llama Guard 3 categories","value":"S1 to S14","note":"Category text is rewritable"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Self-hosting does not remove evaluation","md":"Whichever framework you pick, you own the labelled test set, the thresholds and the upgrade regression runs."}],"whereToUse":["On-premises or sovereign deployments where prompts cannot go to a managed API","Products with a custom safety taxonomy (for example domain-specific advice rules)","Dialog systems that need scripted flows and topic control, not only classification","Python services that need schema validation plus lightweight content checks","Edge or latency-sensitive paths using a small classifier model"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use Llama Guard when you need a self-hosted classifier with rewritable categories","Use NeMo Guardrails when you need rails across input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output","Use Guardrails AI when validators and structured output are the main need","Combine them with deterministic checks for exact rules"],"avoid":["Avoid self-hosting when you cannot run GPUs or maintain an evaluation set","Avoid unpinned framework versions: behaviour changes between releases","Avoid relying on an LLM-prompt rail with a reasoning model without testing the `max_tokens` budget"],"flips":["Data may go to a managed API and categories are standard: use platform guardrails","Image-only moderation is needed: Llama Guard 4 classifies text and image together, so add another image classifier","Policy is mostly exact rules: deterministic checks beat a classifier on cost and clarity"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["NeMo Guardrails","Flow control, topic control, multiple rail types","You want a small dependency or only a classifier","Config learning curve, version churn"],["Guardrails AI","Validators and structured output in Python","You need dialog flow control","Validator quality varies, count changes often"],["Llama Guard (self-hosted)","Custom categories, data stays in house","No GPU capacity, image-only needs","GPU cost, calibration"],["Managed platform guardrails","Standard harm categories, no operations","Custom taxonomy or data residency limits","Language tests are English-centred for several features"],["Prompted LLM judge","Fast to prototype, any policy text","Latency, cost, consistency","Judge bias and drift"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The fact-checking rail blocks every answer after a model change","cause":"A reasoning model used its whole `max_tokens` budget before answering, so the rail failed closed with a 0.0 score","fix":"Raise the budget or use a non-reasoning model for the rail"},{"symptom":"Behaviour changes after a library upgrade","cause":"Framework releases change defaults and prompts (NeMo docs list several releases)","fix":"Pin versions and rerun the evaluation set on every upgrade"},{"symptom":"A rewritten Llama Guard category over-flags or misses","cause":"Category text changed the classifier's behaviour without recalibration","fix":"Evaluate the new taxonomy on labelled data before use"},{"symptom":"A Hub validator passes demos but fails on real data","cause":"Validator quality and coverage vary by language and domain","fix":"Treat validators as components and test them on your own set"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t08-s1","title":"On-premises assistant with a custom policy","situation":"A regulated firm runs an assistant on its own infrastructure. Prompts and documents cannot be sent to an external API. Policy forbids personal investment recommendations, which standard harm categories do not cover.","constraints":["Data stays on premises","Custom policy category","GPU capacity exists","A labelled policy test set can be built"],"unknowns":["How many policy categories?","What latency can the guard add?"],"question":"Which guard approach fits?","options":[{"name":"Self-hosted Llama Guard with rewritten category text, plus deterministic checks for exact rules","verdict":"best","why":"It runs in house and the category text can encode the custom policy.","tradeoff":"GPU operation and the work to calibrate the new category."},{"name":"NeMo Guardrails with Colang flows calling a local model","verdict":"good","why":"Strong flow and topic control without external calls.","tradeoff":"Heavier configuration and an LLM call per rail."},{"name":"A managed content safety API","verdict":"poor","why":"Data leaves the environment and the policy is not a standard category.","tradeoff":"Right when data may be sent and categories are standard."}],"flips":[{"if":"Data may go to a compliant managed service","then":"Use platform guardrails plus a custom classifier for the policy."},{"if":"The policy is a short list of phrases","then":"Deterministic rules are cheaper."}],"takeaway":"Data location and custom taxonomy are the usual reasons to self-host."},{"id":"w06-t08-s2","title":"Python service that must return valid JSON","situation":"A small team runs a Python service that must return schema-valid JSON and block a ban list of terms plus basic PII. They want results this month with little infrastructure.","constraints":["Small team","Python stack","Structured output needed","No GPU capacity"],"unknowns":["Which languages appear?","How strict is PII detection?"],"question":"Which framework do you start with?","options":[{"name":"Guardrails AI with Hub validators for structured output, ban list and PII","verdict":"best","why":"It is a Python library built around input and output guards and structured output, with no GPU needed.","tradeoff":"Validator quality varies, so you test on your data."},{"name":"NeMo Guardrails","verdict":"good","why":"Covers more rail types than this job needs and runs in Python.","tradeoff":"More configuration than this need requires."},{"name":"Train a custom classifier first","verdict":"poor","why":"It delays delivery and needs labelled data and GPUs.","tradeoff":"Right once off-the-shelf validators clearly fail on your data."}],"flips":[{"if":"The product adds multi-turn flows with strict topics","then":"Move to NeMo Guardrails for dialog rails."},{"if":"Self-hosted classification becomes necessary","then":"Add Llama Guard behind a guard."}],"takeaway":"Start with the lightest tool that meets the requirement."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which statement about Llama Guard 4 is correct?","options":["It has 12B parameters, is natively multimodal and runs on one GPU","It classifies an image alone without text","It only works for English text","It is a managed Azure service"],"answer":0,"explain":"Llama Guard 4 is a 12B natively multimodal open-weights model that runs on one GPU. It classifies text and image together, not an image alone, and it is not an Azure service.","ref":"r16"},{"id":"w06-t08-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which are NeMo Guardrails rail types?","options":["Input","Retrieval","Execution","Compression"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"NeMo names input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output rails. Compression is not a rail type.","ref":"r13"},{"id":"w06-t08-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Your NeMo `self check facts` rail suddenly blocks all answers after you switch to a reasoning model. Most likely cause?","options":["The Colang file was deleted","Llama Guard was updated","The model spent its whole `max_tokens` budget thinking, so the rail scored 0.0 and failed closed","The index is stale"],"answer":2,"explain":"The documentation notes the rail fails closed with a 0.0 score when a reasoning model uses its `max_tokens` before answering. The other causes do not match the symptom.","ref":"r14"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Five NeMo Guardrails rail types?","back":"Input, retrieval, dialog, execution and output. Retrieval rails filter chunks, execution rails check tool calls."},{"front":"Guardrails AI in one line?","back":"A Python framework running Input and Output Guards from Hub validators (65 on 2026-10-13), also producing structured output."},{"front":"How do you encode a custom policy in Llama Guard?","back":"Rewrite the category text in the prompt, then evaluate on labelled data. It classifies text and image together, not an image alone."}],"related":["w06-t07","w06-t09","w06-t10","w06-l4","w14-t13"],"refs":["r13","r14","r15","r16","r17","r28","r29"]},{"id":"w06-t09","order":9,"group":"Alternatives and operations","title":"PII detection and redaction","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"PII handling means detecting personal data, then choosing to redact, mask or tokenise it at the right points: ingest, input, output and logs. Detectors are imperfect, so the choice of point and method matters as much as the tool.","useIf":"Prompts, documents, transcripts or logs contain personal data that the model, the index or the log store should not hold.","avoidIf":"You plan to rely on a single output filter: it misses PII that was already sent to the model and written to logs.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Pick the **point** first. At ingest you strip PII before it is embedded (see the ingestion quality-gates topic). On input you remove it before the prompt reaches the model and the logs. On output you filter what the model says. Do all three when the risk justifies it."},{"t":"p","md":"**Azure AI Language PII** has three feature types: text PII (synchronous strings such as prompts, logs and tickets), conversation PII (asynchronous, for transcripts and chats) and document-based PII (asynchronous, native `.pdf`, `.docx` and `.txt`, returning a redacted file plus JSON metadata). Output has categories, confidence scores and redacted text. The Azure AI Search PII skill takes at most 50,000 characters per input [[r18]]. **Presidio** offers an analyzer, an anonymizer, an image redactor and a structured-data module. Its recognizers combine NER, regex and rules with checksums and context, it can call external detectors such as Azure AI Language, and its docs warn there is no guarantee it finds all PII. The project moved to a community-owned Data Privacy Stack with docs at presidio.dataprivacystack.org [[r17]]. The **Foundry PII filter** scans model output only, is preview, follows Azure Language categories, and per category either annotates or annotates and blocks the whole output [[r8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Method.** *Redaction* removes the value. *Masking* overwrites it (for example `****` or a type label). *Tokenisation* swaps it for a placeholder such as `[PERSON_1]` and keeps the mapping in a protected store, so authorised code can restore it in the response. Reversibility is a trade-off: a token vault is itself a sensitive store. If the answer never needs the real value, prefer irreversible redaction. These method distinctions are engineering practice, not product settings."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w06-t09-d1","title":"Reversible redaction around a model call","alt":"User text is scanned for PII and values are replaced with tokens. The tokens go to the model and the mapping goes to a token vault. The response with tokens is restored from the vault before it is shown to the user.","src":"flowchart LR\n  U[User text] --> D[Detect PII]\n  D --> T[Swap for tokens]\n  T --> M[Model]\n  T --> V[(Token vault)]\n  M --> R[Response with tokens]\n  R --> S[Restore values]\n  V --> S\n  S --> A[Answer]"}],"whereToUse":["Customer chat and call-centre transcripts used for analytics or model improvement","RAG ingestion of tickets, HR cases and clinical notes","Prompt logging and tracing, where raw prompts would store personal data","Assistants that must personalise answers without sending raw identifiers to a model","Output review for assistants that might echo personal data"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use irreversible redaction when the value is never needed again","Use tokenisation when the response must restore the value for an authorised user","Use typed placeholders so the model keeps context","Test detectors on your own formats, languages and custom IDs"],"avoid":["Avoid an output-only filter as the sole control: input and logs are already exposed","Avoid asterisk masking where meaning matters: it removes context the model needs","Avoid treating any detector as complete"],"flips":["Data may not leave your environment: use Presidio self-hosted instead of a managed service","Custom identifiers matter: add Presidio recognizers with regex, checksum and context","Documents arrive as files at volume: use the asynchronous document-based mode"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Azure AI Language PII","Managed, many entity types, text, conversation, document modes","Data must stay in house","Per-call cost, residency review"],["Presidio (self-hosted)","Custom recognizers, residency, image and structured data","You cannot maintain it","No guarantee of full coverage, you own tuning"],["Foundry PII filter (output only)","Guard model output with policy actions","PII is already in input or logs","Preview, output only, block action drops the whole output"],["LLM-based detection","Odd formats and context","Cost, latency, consistency","Leaks PII to another model call"],["Regex and checksums","Fixed formats such as card or national IDs","Free-text names and addresses","Easy and cheap, many false negatives"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Personal data appears in prompt logs although output filtering is on","cause":"The Foundry PII filter scans output only, so inputs and traces were never redacted","fix":"Redact before logging and before the model call"},{"symptom":"Answers get worse after masking","cause":"Asterisks removed the type and role of the values","fix":"Use typed placeholders such as `[PERSON_1]`"},{"symptom":"Custom account numbers leak through","cause":"No recognizer for that format and detectors give no coverage guarantee","fix":"Add custom recognizers and test on a labelled sample"},{"symptom":"A token vault breach would expose everything","cause":"The mapping store is broadly accessible and never expires","fix":"Restrict access, separate keys, set expiry and audit reads"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t09-s1","title":"Call-centre transcripts for analytics","situation":"Thousands of recorded call transcripts are processed overnight for trend analysis. Analysts never need names or account numbers. Volume is high and latency does not matter.","constraints":["Batch processing","Values never needed","Transcripts are conversations","Residency allows a managed service"],"unknowns":["Which languages appear?","Are there custom identifiers?"],"question":"How do you handle PII?","options":[{"name":"Irreversible redaction with the asynchronous conversation PII feature before storage","verdict":"best","why":"The mode fits transcripts, runs in batch and leaves no recoverable values.","tradeoff":"Detector misses still leak, so sample and test."},{"name":"Self-hosted Presidio with custom recognizers","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps data in house and supports custom formats.","tradeoff":"You maintain and tune it."},{"name":"Output-only PII filter on the analytics model","verdict":"poor","why":"The transcripts with PII are stored and processed as input, which the output filter never sees.","tradeoff":"Right for guarding what a chat model says."}],"flips":[{"if":"Data may not leave the environment","then":"Presidio self-hosted becomes the best choice."},{"if":"Case handlers need the real values","then":"Tokenise with a vault, not irreversible redaction."}],"takeaway":"Pick the detector mode and the point that match where the data sits."},{"id":"w06-t09-s2","title":"Chat assistant that greets users by name","situation":"A support assistant must write replies that include the customer's name and order number. The team does not want the model provider to receive raw identifiers.","constraints":["Reply needs real values","Model must not see raw IDs","Real-time, interactive","Custom order number format"],"unknowns":["Who may restore values?","How long must mappings live?"],"question":"How do you design redaction?","options":[{"name":"Tokenise before the model call with typed placeholders, restore from a protected vault after, with custom recognizers for order numbers","verdict":"best","why":"The model sees placeholders, the user sees real values, and custom IDs are covered.","tradeoff":"A token vault is a new sensitive store to secure."},{"name":"Mask values with asterisks","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and irreversible.","tradeoff":"The reply cannot contain real values and the model loses context."},{"name":"Send raw text and rely on the output PII filter","verdict":"poor","why":"The raw identifiers already reached the model and logs.","tradeoff":"Right only when the provider is trusted for that data and the filter is an extra layer."}],"flips":[{"if":"Replies never need the real values","then":"Use irreversible redaction and skip the vault."},{"if":"Provider data handling is approved for PII","then":"Keep the output filter as a second layer and simplify inputs."}],"takeaway":"Reversibility is a feature you pay for with a new secret store."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"What does the Foundry PII filter scan?","options":["User input only","Tool responses only","Logs and traces","Model output only"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Foundry PII filter scans model output (completions) only and is preview. It does not cover user input, tool responses or logs, so you need other controls there.","ref":"r8"},{"id":"w06-t09-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each Azure AI Language PII feature to its mode.","pairs":[{"l":"Text PII","r":"Synchronous strings such as prompts and tickets"},{"l":"Conversation PII","r":"Asynchronous transcripts and chats"},{"l":"Document-based PII","r":"Asynchronous `.pdf`, `.docx`, `.txt` with a redacted file"},{"l":"AI Search PII skill","r":"At most 50,000 characters per input"}],"explain":"The three feature types differ by sync or async mode and input shape. The AI Search skill has its own input cap.","ref":"r18"},{"id":"w06-t09-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are benefits of typed placeholders such as `[PERSON_1]` over asterisks?","options":["The model keeps the role of each value","Authorised code can restore real values","No mapping store is needed","Different entities stay distinguishable"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Typed, numbered placeholders keep meaning and allow restoration, but they require a mapping store, which is the cost.","ref":"r17"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is an output-only PII filter not enough?","back":"It scans model output only (preview). Personal data in prompts, tool responses and logs is already exposed."},{"front":"Redaction, masking and tokenisation?","back":"Redaction removes the value, masking overwrites it, tokenisation swaps it for a placeholder and keeps a protected mapping so it can be restored."},{"front":"What does Presidio's documentation warn about?","back":"There is no guarantee it finds all PII. Test your formats and languages, and add custom recognizers."}],"related":["w03-t11","w06-t07","w06-t08","w06-t10","w06-l4","w15-t06"],"refs":["r8","r17","r18"]},{"id":"w06-t10","order":10,"group":"Alternatives and operations","title":"Guard latency, cost and false positives","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Every guard adds latency and cost and blocks some innocent requests. You manage that by running independent guards in parallel, tiering them by risk, and measuring false positives in shadow mode before you enforce.","useIf":"You are adding guards to a live assistant and must keep response time, spend and wrongful blocks inside agreed budgets.","avoidIf":"You treat a guard's accuracy claims as your measured quality: only your own labelled traffic tells you the real error rates.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Latency.** None of the sources read publishes guard latency, so measure it. As an author estimate, a classifier call adds tens to a few hundred milliseconds, and an LLM judge adds a full model call of one to several seconds. Sequential guards add up. Independent guards run in parallel add only the slowest. Streaming adds a choice: default buffering filters each chunk, while the asynchronous filter removes filter latency but signals a stop only within about 1,000 characters [[r9]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Tiering.** Order checks from cheap and deterministic (allow lists, schemas, regular expressions) to managed classifiers to expensive LLM or claim-level checks. Invoke the costly tier only on high-risk routes or borderline scores. Define what happens on a **timeout**: fail closed for high-risk actions, fail open with a logged alert for low-risk ones. Prompt Shields, for example, takes at most five documents per call and 10K characters per prompt, so batching shapes both latency and cost [[r3]] [[r10]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Why most blocks can be false alarms at low base rates","expr":"precision = (TPR x b) / (TPR x b + FPR x (1 - b))","vars":[{"k":"b","v":"Share of requests that are truly harmful (base rate)"},{"k":"TPR","v":"Share of harmful requests the guard catches"},{"k":"FPR","v":"Share of benign requests the guard wrongly flags"}],"example":"With b = 1 percent, TPR = 95 percent and FPR = 2 percent, per 10,000 requests: 95 true blocks and 198 false blocks, so precision is about 32 percent."},{"t":"p","md":"**Measure before you enforce.** Run the guard in shadow mode, log what it would do, and review a sample per category and language. Track precision, recall, block rate, override or appeal rate, p95 added latency and cost per thousand requests. Several Azure features were tested on English only, so slice by language [[r10]]. The Microsoft Learn module on Content Safety and the DeepLearning.AI guardrails course give hands-on practice [[r26]] [[r27]]. Choose the action by the cost of a false positive: block, annotate, repair or escalate."}],"whereToUse":["Setting latency budgets for a RAG or agent request path","Deciding which guards run for every request and which only for risky routes","Launch reviews: shadow-mode results before turning on blocking","Monitoring: block-rate and override-rate dashboards with alerts on drift","Cost reviews where LLM-based guards double the model spend"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use parallel execution for independent input guards and retrieval","Use tiering so expensive checks see only risky or borderline traffic","Use shadow mode and labelled sets to measure precision and recall per language","Use annotate or repair actions when false positives would be costly"],"avoid":["Avoid chaining independent guards in sequence","Avoid enforcing a new guard before measuring its false positive rate","Avoid one global threshold for all languages and categories"],"flips":["Harm per miss is very high and volume is low: run every guard sequentially and accept the latency","Latency budget is under one second: keep deterministic checks inline and push model-based checks to async review","False positives dominate complaints: tune thresholds per category or change the action from block to annotate"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Execution pattern","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Sequential guards","Low volume, high risk, relaxed latency","Tight latency","Latency is the sum of all guards"],["Parallel guards","Independent checks, tight latency","A later guard depends on an earlier result","Latency is the slowest guard, wasted work when one blocks"],["Tiered guards","Mixed risk traffic, cost sensitive","Everything is high risk","Routing logic and borderline-score tuning"],["Asynchronous or buffered output filter","Streaming UX","Zero tolerance for brief exposure (async)","Async shows text before the stop signal"],["Post-hoc audit only","Low risk analytics","Immediate harm","Detects after the fact only"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Block rate jumps after a platform or model update","cause":"The guard model or its thresholds changed behaviour","fix":"Alert on block-rate drift, replay a labelled set after updates and shadow new versions first"},{"symptom":"p95 latency grows although guards run in parallel","cause":"The slowest guard sets the tail and has no timeout policy","fix":"Set per-guard timeouts with fail-closed or fail-open rules by risk"},{"symptom":"Users complain about wrongful refusals in one language","cause":"Thresholds were tuned on English traffic","fix":"Measure per-language precision and tune or change action per language"},{"symptom":"Guard spend rivals model spend","cause":"An LLM judge runs on every request","fix":"Tier: run it only on high-risk or borderline cases"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w06-t10-s1","title":"Voice assistant with a 1.5 second budget","situation":"A voice assistant must start speaking within 1.5 seconds. It uses retrieval and a model. Harm is moderate and responses are short.","constraints":["1.5 second latency budget","Short outputs","Moderate harm","Retrieval takes about 400 ms"],"unknowns":["How slow is each guard at p95?","Is a stop mid-utterance acceptable?"],"question":"How do you arrange the guards?","options":[{"name":"Deterministic checks inline, input classifier in parallel with retrieval, buffered output filter, LLM judge only on audited samples","verdict":"best","why":"Parallel input checks hide behind retrieval and short outputs make buffering cheap.","tradeoff":"Claim-level checks happen offline, not in the loop."},{"name":"Run every guard sequentially including an LLM judge","verdict":"poor","why":"A judge adds seconds, breaking the budget.","tradeoff":"Right for a low-volume, high-risk text workflow with relaxed latency."},{"name":"Skip guards and audit afterwards","verdict":"good","why":"Zero added latency.","tradeoff":"Harm reaches users before detection, acceptable only for very low risk."}],"flips":[{"if":"Budget relaxes to 4 seconds","then":"Add an LLM or NLI check on high-risk intents."},{"if":"Harm per miss rises sharply","then":"Buffer output and accept more latency."}],"takeaway":"Hide cheap guards behind work you already do, and push heavy ones off the critical path."},{"id":"w06-t10-s2","title":"Public chat with a 0.5 percent harmful rate","situation":"A high-volume consumer chat sees about one harmful request in 200. A classifier with 95 percent recall and a 2 percent false positive rate is proposed in blocking mode.","constraints":["High volume","Base rate 0.5 percent","Guard TPR 95 percent, FPR 2 percent","Wrongful blocks drive complaints"],"unknowns":["What does a wrongful block cost in churn?","Can users appeal?"],"question":"How do you roll it out?","options":[{"name":"Shadow mode first, review sampled blocks per category and language, then tune thresholds and use annotate or step-up friction for borderline scores","verdict":"best","why":"At 0.5 percent base rate most blocks would hit benign users, so measure and soften the action first.","tradeoff":"A delay before full enforcement."},{"name":"Enable blocking immediately","verdict":"poor","why":"About 4 of 5 blocks would be wrong: 0.5 percent of 10,000 is 50 harmful, 47.5 caught, versus 199 false blocks.","tradeoff":"Right when the base rate is high or harm per miss is extreme."},{"name":"Raise the threshold until no complaints remain","verdict":"good","why":"Quickly reduces false positives.","tradeoff":"Recall drops and you will not know by how much without a labelled set."}],"flips":[{"if":"Harm per miss is severe (self-harm content to minors)","then":"Accept more false positives and add human escalation."},{"if":"The base rate is 20 percent","then":"Precision rises sharply and blocking is more defensible."}],"takeaway":"Compute precision from the base rate before you choose block."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w06-t10-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Base rate 1 percent, TPR 95 percent, FPR 2 percent. Of all blocks, what percentage are true positives (precision)?","answer":32,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"percent","hint":"Per 10,000 requests: 95 true blocks, 198 false blocks.","explain":"True blocks: 10,000 x 1% x 95% = 95. False blocks: 9,900 x 2% = 198. Precision = 95 / 293 = about 32 percent.","ref":"r1"},{"id":"w06-t10-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Three independent guards of 100 ms, 200 ms and 300 ms run in parallel. What is the added latency?","options":["About 300 ms","About 600 ms","About 100 ms","About 200 ms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Parallel independent guards add about the slowest: 300 ms. Sequential execution would add 600 ms.","ref":"r9"},{"id":"w06-t10-q3","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which practices reduce cost and wrongful blocks together?","options":["Tier guards so LLM judges see only risky or borderline traffic","Shadow mode before enforcing","One global threshold for every language","Per-language precision measurement"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Tiering, shadow mode and per-language measurement address cost and false positives. A single global threshold hides language-specific error rates.","ref":"r10"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why do most blocks hit benign users at low base rates?","back":"Benign traffic dwarfs harmful traffic, so even a 2% false positive rate produces more false blocks than true ones when harm is about 1%."},{"front":"What does tiering guards mean?","back":"Order checks from cheap deterministic ones to classifiers to LLM judges, running costly ones only for high-risk or borderline cases."},{"front":"What is shadow mode?","back":"Run the guard and log its decisions without acting, so you can measure false positives per category and language before enforcing."}],"related":["w06-t01","w06-t02","w06-t07","w06-l5","w14-t13","w16-t10"],"refs":["r1","r3","r9","r10","r26","r27"]}]});
